# claude-fleet configuration — copy to <install-dir>/fleet.conf and edit.
# Sourced by the fleet scripts (plain shell; keep it assignments-only).
#
# TAG LINES (the "# @label=… @group=… @tier=… @scope=… @edit=… @unit=…" line
# directly above each key) are the DECLARATIVE source of truth for the prefix+c
# config modal (issue #89). One reviewable file drives the whole UI — there is
# no hardcoded key list in the scripts. Each key carries three independent
# attributes:
#   @scope  which file holds it:  identity (view-only) · global (the login's
#           ~/.config/claude-fleet/fleet.settings; stripped from a fleet conf) ·
#           fleet (the fleet's conf). To you both are "this fleet" — one login
#           runs one fleet — so the modal's ⌃s only picks THIS FLEET ⇄ a hosted
#           REPO (issue #1102) and routes each key to its file itself. This
#           install fleet.conf is still read, as a legacy layer under both,
#           but the modal never writes it.
#   @tier   modal visibility:     common (default view) · advanced (collapsed) ·
#           internal (pacing/budget/timeout knobs, issue #1101: NOT listed by the
#           modal or `fcfg_table` unless you open "show all" — the modal's
#           INTERNAL header, or FLEET_CONFIG_SHOW_INTERNAL=1. Still read at
#           runtime, still hand-overridable in any conf; defaults stay here).
#   @edit   editor + validation:  no · bool · int · enum · path · str · regex.
# @label is the friendly name shown in the modal; @group buckets rows into
# sections; @unit is an optional display suffix. Adding a key here (with a tag
# line) makes it appear in the modal automatically — nothing to keep in sync.

# ── Identity ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# These pin WHICH repo/checkout this fleet is. They are view-only in the modal
# (@edit=no): change them by editing fleet.conf and re-provisioning, not live.

# The GitHub repo whose issues are your backlog (owner/name). Required for the
# backlog panel, issue-bound sessions, and PR/CI cells.
# @label=GitHub repo @group=identity @tier=common @scope=identity @edit=no
FLEET_REPO="you/your-repo"

# Main checkout of that repo — issue worktrees are created as SIBLINGS of this
# directory (your-repo-issue-123 next to your-repo).
# @label=Base checkout path @group=identity @tier=common @scope=identity @edit=no
FLEET_MAIN="$HOME/projects/your-repo"

# Branch new work forks from (and merged-ness is measured against).
# @label=Base branch (locked) @group=identity @tier=common @scope=identity @edit=no
FLEET_BASE_BRANCH="main"

# Directory holding the per-fleet config overlays (<session>.conf), which the
# modal writes when its scope is set to per-fleet. Defaults to
# $HOME/.config/claude-fleet; override only if you relocate the fleet state dir.
# @label=Per-fleet config dir @group=identity @tier=internal @scope=identity @edit=no
#FLEET_CONF_DIR="$HOME/.config/claude-fleet"

# A repo's short tag in a fleet that hosts 2+ repos (issue #793): the dash badge
# under "all" and the window-name prefix (`tl·issue-12`). Set it in the repo's own
# conf — this fleet conf for its repo, repos/<slug>.conf for any other. Empty = the
# name's initials (claude-fleet → cf), or its first two letters (tokenledger → to).
# @label=Repo short tag @group=identity @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=no
#FLEET_REPO_SHORT=""

# The login's SEED repo (issue #1167): `fleet-up.sh --seed` brings a new login's
# fleet up on a starter repo (claude-fleet itself) so it works at once — and that
# repo only LOOKS. 1 = the dispatcher never autofills it and the issue-bridge never
# relays its comments, whatever FLEET_AUTOFILL / FLEET_ISSUE_BRIDGE say. Marks the
# fleet conf's OWN repo only: a repo added later is never the seed. fleet-up writes
# it (with FLEET_AUTOFILL=0 + FLEET_ISSUE_BRIDGE=0); readers ask fleet_repo_is_seed.
# Once the login hosts a repo of its own, `fleet-repo.sh remove <seed>` takes the
# starter out: that repo is promoted into this slot and the seed keys go (#1172).
# @label=Seed repo (look only) @group=identity @tier=advanced @scope=identity @edit=no
#FLEET_SEED=0

# --- Public SSH entry (issue #1195) ---------------------------------------
# How a NEWCOMER reaches this machine from outside — the host and port that the
# welcome letter fleet-login-new.sh writes (~/<login>-onboard/welcome.txt, EPIC
# #1212) puts in its `ssh -p <port> <login>@<host>` line and ssh-config snippet.
# Machine-wide, so global: every login opened on this box is reached the same way.
# Unset host ⇒ the letter carries a visible `<HOST>` placeholder and the run WARNs;
# unset port ⇒ 22 (ssh's own default). Also what `fleet-doctor`'s `ingress` line
# probes from outside (issue #1196, next key).
# @label=Public SSH host @group=identity @tier=common @scope=global @edit=str
#FLEET_SSH_PUBLIC_HOST=ssh.example.com
# @label=Public SSH port @group=identity @tier=common @scope=global @edit=int
#FLEET_SSH_PUBLIC_PORT=22

# Probe host for the public entry (issue #1196). An ssh destination on the FAR
# side of the internet — an alias in ~/.ssh/config with key auth and its host key
# already known, e.g. a VPS — from which `fleet-doctor`'s `ingress` line runs
# `ssh-keyscan -p <port> <host>` against the two keys above and compares the
# ed25519 host key with sshd's on 127.0.0.1 here: the entry is open AND lands on
# THIS machine. From this machine or the same LAN that cannot be told (NAT
# hairpin) — which is how a newcomer's port was found only from a box abroad.
# Unset ⇒ no `ingress` line at all. Machine-wide.
# @label=Public-entry probe host @group=identity @tier=common @scope=global @edit=str
#FLEET_SSH_PROBE_HOST=my-vps

# First-fleet guide (issue #1169). The first time this login's fleet is created,
# fleet-up opens the /fleet-onboard wizard as a `guide` scratch pinned to the top
# of the session list, and writes $FLEET_CONF_DIR/global/onboarded only after the
# guide has SPOKEN — its `fleet-onboard.sh brief` left global/guide.spoke (issue
# #1215) — and its agent is still running. A first attempt that died, or that
# came up and only printed `Unknown command`, is retried by the collector: a dead
# guide after FLEET_GUIDE_COOLDOWN, a running-but-silent one after
# FLEET_GUIDE_SPEAK_SECS. Only a brand-new fleet enables this — an existing fleet
# without the marker does not. Set to 0 to create fleets without it. GLOBAL
# (read before any fleet exists).
# @label=Open the guide on a first fleet @group=spawn @tier=advanced @scope=global @edit=bool
#FLEET_ONBOARD=1
# @label=Guide startup wait @group=spawn @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_GUIDE_WAIT_SECS=30
# @label=Guide retry cooldown @group=spawn @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_GUIDE_COOLDOWN=60
# @label=Guide grace to speak before restart @group=spawn @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_GUIDE_SPEAK_SECS=180
# After this many opens that never spoke the collector stops and writes the reason
# to global/onboard.stuck (doctor's onboard row, the guide window); `fleet guide`
# gives it a fresh budget (issue #2424).
# @label=Guide tries before it stops @group=spawn @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int
#FLEET_GUIDE_MAX_TRIES=3

# ── Per-repo settings (issue #978) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# A fleet hosting 2+ repos keeps each further repo's conf at
# fleets/<session>/repos/<slug>.conf (bin/fleet-repo.sh add). Beyond identity
# (FLEET_REPO / FLEET_MAIN / FLEET_BASE_BRANCH / FLEET_REPO_SHORT), the deploy knobs
# (FLEET_DEPLOY_REF / FLEET_DEPLOY_CHECK) and the launch knobs (FLEET_MODEL /
# FLEET_AGENT / FLEET_MCP_CONFIG), these keys are honored PER REPO — set one in a
# repo's overlay and that repo's windows, worktrees and daemons passes use it; leave
# it unset there and the repo gets the fleet conf's value (then this file's):
#   per-repo: FLEET_WORKTREE_SETUP FLEET_WORKTREE_SETUP_TIMEOUT FLEET_BASE_DEPS FLEET_SLEEP_MCP_RESTARTABLE FLEET_SCRATCH_POOL FLEET_ISSUE_BRIDGE FLEET_CLEANUP FLEET_AUTOFILL FLEET_WEBHOOK
# (That line is kept in lockstep with _FLEET_REPO_OVERRIDABLE in bin/fleet-lib.sh
# by bin/repo-settings-selftest.sh.) Setting one to "" in an overlay switches it
# off for that repo. `fleet-repo.sh get <KEY> <owner/repo>` prints what a repo sees.
# A fleet with no repos/ overlay reads every key exactly as it always has.

# Branches the worktree janitor must never touch (extended regex).
# @label=Protected-branch pattern @group=janitor @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=regex
FLEET_PROTECTED_RE="^(master|main|develop|test)$"

# --- Follow the stable mark automatically (issue #1120, EPIC #1117) --------------
# ON by default, per LOGIN. Every 30 min com.claude-fleet.install-sync
# (bin/fleet-install-sync.sh) fetches refs/tags/stable from the public repo (https,
# no credentials) and, when this login's HEAD is behind it, checks the new
# version out beside the old one (~/.claude/fleet.versions/<sha>/), checks it,
# switches the ~/.claude/fleet LINK to it in one rename — busy sessions and a
# running EPIC batch no longer hold it back (issue #1894): a running script keeps
# its files, the next call reads the new ones — then runs
# bin/fleet-install-apply.sh (daemons reloaded, hooks merged, commands/skills
# installed) and bin/fleet-doctor.sh; a NEW doctor FAIL line switches the link
# back and that version is not retried until stable moves. It never moves
# backward, never touches an install with tracked local changes, and waits while
# the disk gate is closed. A plain-directory install is moved into
# ~/.claude/fleet.versions/ at its first switch.
# When it gets STUCK — refused, rolled back, or deferred past
# FLEET_INSTALL_FOLLOW_STUCK_SECS — the doctor's install row WARNs, and the tick
# that turned stuck sends ONE FLEET_NOTIFY_CMD message (issue #1125), deduped on
# login + reason + stable and re-armed once the login follows again; a failed
# fetch (offline) never alarms. Set to 0 to switch it off for this login —
# no following, no warning, no message (a hand /fleet-sync-install still
# works; fleet-sync-logins.sh — the last step of /fleet-sync-install, issue #1122 — skips a
# login that opted out unless named, or --include-off). State:
# $FLEET_CONF_DIR/global/install-sync.state; log: logs/install-sync.log.
# @label=Follow stable automatically @group=janitor @tier=common @scope=global @edit=bool
#FLEET_INSTALL_SYNC=1
# How long one fetch of refs/tags/stable may stall before the tick gives up and
# records `fetch-failed` (git's own low-speed abort; the tag is fetched once per
# tick over https). Not seen ≠ refused: nothing moves, nothing alarms. GLOBAL.
# @label=Install-sync fetch timeout @group=janitor @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=seconds
#FLEET_INSTALL_SYNC_TIMEOUT=30
# The node agent follows stable too (issue #1723): once the install IS stable, the
# same tick upgrades this machine's ccquota agent to prod-<stable short> when it
# is behind — the hub's /v1/node/dist binary first, a local build else; one login
# at a time, confirmed on the hub, rolled back to its .prev when it does not come
# up. A LaunchDaemon login needs sudo -n, so the admin login's tick does those.
# A failure sends ONE FLEET_NOTIFY_CMD per (login, stable). 0 = never touch the
# agent from this login's tick (fleet-node-upgrade.sh by hand still works). GLOBAL.
# @label=Upgrade the node agent with stable @group=janitor @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=bool
#FLEET_NODE_FOLLOW=1
# After a failed agent upgrade, the same stable is not retried for this long
# (a stable move retries at once). GLOBAL.
# @label=Node agent retry after failure @group=janitor @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=seconds
#FLEET_NODE_FOLLOW_RETRY_SECS=21600
# A /loop session parked between rounds (`looping`, waiting on nothing but its
# Loop) defers the node-agent upgrade (the install switch no longer waits) only while its next round — a ScheduleWakeup or a
# CronCreate job — may start within this many seconds (issue #1690); a cron
# that fires tomorrow morning no longer pins the install overnight. A round
# whose time is unknown (a mark written before #1690) still defers. GLOBAL.
# @label=Install-sync loop margin @group=janitor @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=seconds
#FLEET_INSTALL_LOOP_MARGIN_SECS=600
# How long a login may sit `deferred` (the disk gate) — or go without any
# install-sync tick at all — before the doctor's install row and
# fleet-install-follow.sh call it STUCK (WARN) instead of following normally.
# A day: a session busy that long is usually a stuck one, and a daemon silent
# that long is not ticking. GLOBAL; read as a plain seconds count.
# @label=Install-follow stuck threshold @group=janitor @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=seconds
#FLEET_INSTALL_FOLLOW_STUCK_SECS=86400
# How long a version the install switched away from stays in
# ~/.claude/fleet.versions/ (issue #1894) — a long-running process started from
# it (an MCP server that resolved its own path) keeps working until then. The
# current version, .prev and the checkout holding the repository are never
# removed. GLOBAL; seconds.
# @label=Keep retired install versions @group=janitor @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=seconds
#FLEET_INSTALL_VERSIONS_KEEP_SECS=604800
# ← in a Claude pane opens the agents view (and its prompt hint says
# "← for agents"); a stray press strands you there. The sync fills
# leftArrowOpensAgents=false into this login's ~/.claude.json — Claude Code's
# global config, the only place that key is read — from the fleet's default
# Claude configuration, conf/claude-settings.default.json (issue #1558, which
# folded #1528's key in); the doctor's `settings` row counts every default key
# that differs. The fill never overwrites a value the login set; set this to 1
# to leave the key alone entirely (the same as listing it in
# ~/.claude/settings.fleet-override.json). GLOBAL.
# @label=Keep ← opening the agents view @group=janitor @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=bool
#FLEET_KEEP_AGENTS_KEY=0

# --- Orphan-process reap for KEPT worktrees (issue #469) ------------------------
# ON by default, GLOBALLY. #151 already reaps a worktree's detached processes when
# the janitor PRUNES it. But a dirty/unmerged worktree is KEPT forever (a scratch
# experiment must never be silently deleted — #290), so before #469 its dev/mock
# servers ran on indefinitely: eleven were found alive two DAYS after their window
# closed, two of them pegging a core. The janitor now also sweeps a worktree it is
# KEEPING, but only past the liveness gate — i.e. only once no window is bound to
# it. A PROTECTED worktree never reaches that gate and is never touched. Set to 0
# if you deliberately leave a preview server running behind a closed window.
# @label=Reap orphan procs of kept worktrees @group=janitor @tier=advanced @scope=global @edit=bool
#FLEET_REAP_KEPT_PROCS=1
# Minimum process age (seconds) for that recurring sweep. The reaper also matches
# argv, so a live session's transient command that merely NAMES the worktree path
# must not be eligible; only something that has genuinely settled in is. Prune-time
# reaping is unaffected (the directory is going away anyway). Default 600.
# @label=Kept-worktree reap min age @group=janitor @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=seconds
#FLEET_REAP_KEPT_MINAGE=600

# --- Idle scratch reclaim (issue #884) ------------------------------------------
# OFF by default (0 = the #290 behaviour, byte for byte). A clean scratch worktree
# where a real session ran is kept FOREVER today — the conversation is the work, so
# no git-state rule may reap it — and they pile up: 53 of 92 worktrees on one
# machine had not moved in 3 days, each costing disk, file-watcher load and a
# collector scan. Set seconds (259200 = 3 days is the recommended value): once the
# last session activity (newest human transcript, else the dir mtime) is older,
# the hourly janitor drops a CLEAN scratch (strict ancestor, or tip == base) into
# .fleet-trash — recoverable, /fleet-history row recorded first, and only past
# every liveness gate (live pane / rotation lease / live procs). A DIRTY or
# UNMERGED scratch is never deleted: past the age it joins ONE digest notify, at
# most every 24h, listing all of them. Log line: `autoclean: scratch idle <Nd> →
# trash <path>`. Per fleet.
# @label=Reclaim idle scratch after @group=janitor @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=int @unit=seconds
#FLEET_SCRATCH_MAX_IDLE=0

# --- Rotation lease TTL (issue #550) -------------------------------------------
# An account rotation (bin/fleet-migrate.sh) closes a walled window and opens its
# replacement seconds later. Through that gap the worktree has no window and no
# @issue binding, so it reads to every timer-driven reaper exactly like a worker
# that finished — the janitor hit one on 2026-09-11 and swept all 15 processes of
# a live worker. The mover now takes a LEASE on the worktree for the duration and
# the reapers leave a leased worktree alone. This is how long an unreleased lease
# stays believed: long enough for the slowest move (exit wait + cold boot), short
# enough that a mover killed mid-move cannot park a worktree forever. Default 900.
# @label=Rotation lease TTL @group=janitor @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=seconds
#FLEET_ROTATE_LEASE_TTL=900

# --- Helper `claude -p` calls carry no MCP (issue #468) -------------------------
# ON by default, GLOBALLY. bin/classify-sessions.sh reads a pane and answers in one
# word (the dash summarizer that shared this flag retired in issue #535). Left unflagged, EVERY such call boots the
# operator's whole MCP set — 4+ node children, the remote connectors, and a cold `rg`
# scan: measured at 5.2s and up to 517MB RSS, on every Stop hook in every window. They
# now pass --strict-mcp-config with an empty config (auth and settings stay intact,
# unlike --bare, which drops the login). Set to 0 to restore the unflagged calls if a
# future CLI changes what those flags mean — both call sites swallow stderr, so a
# silent breakage would simply stop the summaries.
# @label=Strip MCP from helper claude -p @group=status @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=bool
#FLEET_HELPER_NO_MCP=1

# Context window used for the dash's ctx% column (tokens). 200000 for standard
# plans; 1000000 if your sessions run 1M-context models. Also the FALLBACK
# denominator bin/fleet-context.sh (/fleet-context) uses when it DERIVES a
# percentage for a pane with no @ctx_limit stamp (issue #477; conf/statusline.sh
# stamps the real size every render, so a fleet running it needs no value here).
# FLEET_CONTEXT_LIMIT is the retired twin of this key (issue #1101): still read,
# and it wins over this one when set, so an old conf keeps working unchanged.
# @label=Context window size @group=cache @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=int @unit=tokens
FLEET_CTX_WINDOW=200000

# Model every spawned fleet session launches with (passed as `claude --model`).
# Default: opus — so a new window never silently falls back to sonnet. Set empty
# to defer to your own `claude` default; an explicit --model in the spawn still
# wins. Honored globally here or per-fleet in $FLEET_CONF_DIR/<session>.conf.
# The prefix+c config modal edits this as an fzf picker of the CLI's latest-of-tier
# aliases — opus, sonnet, haiku, fable, opusplan, default — plus a custom claude-*
# entry, so you choose instead of type.
# @label=Worker model @group=spawn @tier=common @scope=fleet @edit=enum
#FLEET_MODEL="opus"

# Model the session's SUBAGENTS (Task/Agent spawns) run on, exported as
# CLAUDE_CODE_SUBAGENT_MODEL — the only global switch for subagent models (there
# is no settings.json key). Overrides even the pinned built-ins (claude-code-guide
# runs haiku, statusline-setup runs sonnet by default). Defaults to FLEET_MODEL
# (so subagents follow the session onto opus). Set to "inherit" to let each
# subagent resolve normally, or empty to leave the env var untouched. The config
# modal offers the same alias picker as FLEET_MODEL, plus an `inherit` entry.
# @label=Subagent model @group=spawn @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=enum
#FLEET_SUBAGENT_MODEL="opus"

# The fleet's ONE orchestrating session (issue #1957, bin/fleet-orchestrator.sh):
# a no-repo session in $HOME that lives behind the client's 「新任务」 — the person
# hands it a draft (⇧⇥ in the writing area), it talks the work through and then
# files / spawns / plans it (skills/fleet-orchestrate/). fleet-up opens it, the
# diskguard tick reopens it when it is closed. Default: on where this computer
# hosts sessions (FLEET_HOST=1, 承载); 0 = no orchestrator on this fleet.
# @label=Orchestrator session @group=spawn @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=bool
#FLEET_ORCHESTRATOR=1

# ⌘N on the client (its [client] section — issue #2616): 0 (default) = straight
# to the orchestrating session's input, woken first through the hub
# (orch_ensure) when no machine runs it, at most FLEET_ORCH_BOOT_SECS; 1 = the
# writing area on the right as before (multi-line, attachments, ⇧⇥ hands the
# draft to the orchestrator). Read at each ⌘N; the bar's word at the shell's start.
# @label=⌘N opens the writing area @group=status @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=bool
#FLEET_COMPOSE=0
# @label=Orchestrator wake wait @group=status @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=seconds
#FLEET_ORCH_BOOT_SECS=75

# The orchestrator runs this login's default agent (FLEET_AGENT) on its strongest
# model at high effort: Claude --model FLEET_ORCH_MODEL (fable; FLEET_MODEL_FALLBACK
# while that model is capped on the active account) --effort FLEET_ORCH_EFFORT;
# Codex -m FLEET_ORCH_CODEX_MODEL (empty: the login's own) with
# model_reasoning_effort = FLEET_ORCH_EFFORT. Read when it is (re)opened.
# @label=Orchestrator model (Claude) @group=spawn @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=str
#FLEET_ORCH_MODEL="fable"
# @label=Orchestrator model (Codex) @group=spawn @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=str
#FLEET_ORCH_CODEX_MODEL=""
# @label=Orchestrator effort @group=spawn @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=str
#FLEET_ORCH_EFFORT="high"
# A Claude orchestrator that exited (/exit, a crash, a killed process) waits on the
# recovery page this many seconds, then the next tick resumes the same conversation
# in place with a first turn that re-arms its Loop (issue #2585). `off` keeps the page.
# @label=Orchestrator revive after (s) @group=spawn @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=str
#FLEET_ORCH_REVIVE_SECS=30

# When an account hits a PER-MODEL cap ("You've hit your Fable 5 limit · resets
# Sep 6 …" / "You've reached your Fable limit …") while its subscription still
# has 5h/7d headroom, the fleet does NOT bench the account (that rotation is for
# the subscription wall — benching on a model cap moved every session onto an
# account with the same cap). It records the (account, model) cap until the
# banner's reset, relaunches the walled sessions on THIS model (close + `--resume
# --model`, same transcript), and every new session on that account launches on
# it until the cap resets — autofill, hand spawns, restores alike; subagents
# follow. Set empty to disable (a model cap then behaves like a subscription
# wall: bench + rotate). Machine-wide policy: one value for every fleet.
# @label=Model-cap fallback @group=spawn @tier=common @scope=global @edit=enum
#FLEET_MODEL_FALLBACK="opus"

# How long a per-model cap stays recorded when its banner carries no parseable
# reset instant (the sticky "reached your Fable limit" line has none). Model caps
# are weekly, so the default is 7 days (seconds); a banner WITH a reset wins.
# @label=Model-cap TTL @group=spawn @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_MODEL_LIMIT_TTL=604800

# Per-model utilization (%) at which ccquota's per-model window (the Fable cap,
# `models` in `ccquota budget --json`, issue #1073) counts as CAPPED even while its
# status still reads allowed. Every quota fetch seeds the model-cap ledger from it,
# with ccquota's REAL reset — so a spawn picks an account that still has headroom
# for FLEET_MODEL (or launches on the fallback) before any session hits the wall.
# Default 100: only a full window. An older ccquota without `models` changes nothing.
# @label=Model-cap threshold @group=spawn @tier=advanced @scope=global @edit=int @unit=%
#FLEET_MODEL_CAP_PCT=100

# SYSTEM-WIDE ceiling on how many Claude working sessions may run at once across
# ALL fleets on this machine. Every spawn path — the new-session box and the
# backlog's Enter — refuses once this many are live (panels — the dash/plan/
# backlog windows — don't count). Default 0 = off (issue #1831): a per-login number
# does not add up across logins and the real limit is memory, so the machine
# admission below decides. Set one only to cap hard.
# @label=Max sessions — all fleets @group=caps @tier=common @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sessions
FLEET_GLOBAL_MAX_SESSIONS=0

# MACHINE-WIDE ceiling across every LOGIN on this box (issue #1301). The cap above
# counts only THIS login's fleets; on a shared machine five logins at 36 each add
# up to nothing. Each login's collector writes its live count (+ in-flight spawns)
# once a tick to /Users/Shared/claude-fleet/sessions/<login> (1777, never under a
# $HOME); a spawn refuses when this login's live count + the others' files reach
# the ceiling, and the refusal lists every login's share. A file older than
# FLEET_MACHINE_SESSIONS_STALE seconds counts 0. Default 0 = unlimited (no file is
# read); `fleet-doctor`'s `machine` line states the total either way — read a
# week of peaks there before picking a number. Crash restore, quota migration and
# handoff re-house running sessions and never pass any cap.
# @label=Max sessions — all logins @group=caps @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sessions
#FLEET_MACHINE_MAX_SESSIONS=0
# @label=Machine session file stale after @group=caps @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=s
#FLEET_MACHINE_SESSIONS_STALE=300

# PER-FLEET ceiling on concurrent Claude working sessions in THIS fleet, enforced
# alongside the global cap by every spawn path (a spawn needs BOTH caps to have
# headroom). Default 0 = unlimited (only the global cap applies) — set it to give
# one repo its own slice of the global budget. Best set per-fleet in
# $FLEET_CONF_DIR/<session>.conf; a value here applies to every fleet.
# @label=Max sessions — this fleet @group=caps @tier=common @scope=fleet @edit=int @unit=sessions
#FLEET_MAX_SESSIONS=0

# --- MCP allowlist for spawned sessions (bin/fleet-claude.sh, issue #473) -------
# Unset by default = UNCHANGED: a session boots every MCP server the operator has
# configured. That is the right default and the wrong bill for most fleets — a
# 30-day census of every mcp__* call across 4690 transcripts on one machine found
# all of it in three servers (playwright 426, seedance 36, mcp-image 31) used by ONE
# fleet, while the other fleet made ZERO MCP calls and still paid ~2s of startup and
# 4-5 resident node children per session for thirteen servers. Being @scope=fleet,
# this lets each fleet pay only for what it uses. Values:
#   (unset/empty)  every configured MCP server loads — today's behaviour
#   none           no MCP server at all
#   <path|JSON>    ONLY these servers; the CLI takes a file path OR inline JSON
# RECOMMENDED (issue #1078): point it at the minimal worker set the fleet ships,
#   (e.g.)  FLEET_MCP_CONFIG="$HOME/.claude/fleet/conf/mcp-worker.json"
# — just the fleet's own tool service `fleet` (issue #1807), as a FILE: a fleet
# that needs another server copies it to its own conf dir, adds what it needs
# (docs/INSTALL.md "MCP servers on demand" has the common add-backs), and
# points this key there. On a machine running dozens of sessions every server is
# one resident process per session, so this is the biggest per-session saving.
# --strict-mcp-config is what also drops the REMOTE (claude.ai connector) servers,
# not just local stdio ones. An explicit --mcp-config/--strict-mcp-config from the
# caller always wins. Trade-off worth knowing: a census measures what was USED, not
# what may be NEEDED — a trimmed fleet session won't have the server it never asked
# for before. It takes effect on the next spawned session, and plain `claude` outside
# a fleet is untouched, so the full set is always one ordinary session away.
# Codex workers read the same value unless FLEET_CODEX_MCP_CONFIG is set (below).
# @label=MCP servers for this fleet @group=spawn @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=str
#FLEET_MCP_CONFIG=""

# The fleet's own tool service (issue #1807, docs/FLEET-MCP.md): every Claude and
# Codex session mounts bin/fleet-mcp.py as the MCP server `fleet` — status ·
# children · repos · agents · spawn · await · send, the same tools for both
# agents — beside whatever FLEET_MCP_CONFIG chose (`none` included). 0 = don't
# mount it: the launch argv is byte for byte what it was before #1807.
# @label=Fleet tool service @group=spawn @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=bool
#FLEET_MCP=1

# Which agent CLI a spawned session runs (issue #547). Every spawn path — the
# backlog Enter / `▸ issue`, the dash ⌃s / prompt-line scratch, autofill, the
# issue-bridge revive — funnels through bin/fleet-claude.sh, which branches here:
#   claude   (default) Claude Code, exactly as before — the default fleet's spawn
#            argv is byte-for-byte unchanged.
#   codex    OpenAI Codex CLI (`codex`, 0.154+), via bin/fleet-codex.sh. Modern
#            Codex reads native skills from $CODEX_HOME/skills, so a bare
#            `/fleet-claim` seed becomes `$fleet-claim` when that home has been
#            synced; old Codex / unsynced homes still fall back to the prose
#            prompt (conf/codex-preamble.md + commands/fleet-claim.md). The
#            fleet's Stop / PostToolUse / PreToolUse hooks ride inline as `-c hooks.*`
#            (dash state + the bash-guard and base-checkout guard — Codex's hook
#            schema is Claude Code's); CLAUDE.md is read as the project doc; and
#            Claude model aliases and OAuth tokens stay Claude-only; MCP policy
#            is translated and Codex has native skills/subagent knobs. Codex auth is `codex login`, not the
#            fleet's account files. Runs full-access (`--dangerously-bypass-
#            approvals-and-sandbox`), the same posture as a bypassPermissions Claude
#            worker: the hooks are the rails.
# The dash prompt line shows the EFFECTIVE value — `claude ▸ ` / `codex ▸ ` — and
# ⌃v in the dash flips it here, in this fleet's conf (issue #554), so every spawn
# path follows and the dash shows the truth.
# Pick it with the dash toggle key (⌃v, or its ⌥ twin when ⌃v is your tmux
# prefix — the `?` sheet shows the real one), prefix+c, or this key. There is NO
# prompt-line prefix (issue #559): `codex: fix parser` typed on the dash is a
# scratch NAMED "codex: fix parser" on the fleet's default agent. Per-spawn
# override for scripts: `--agent codex|claude` on dash-issue-session.sh /
# dash-raw-session.sh. A `--resume` /
# `--continue` / `--from-pr` launch without an explicit agent resumes Claude
# history. Saved Codex sessions pass their provider and home explicitly. What a Codex worker does and does NOT
# get is graded row by row in README.md's "Agents: Claude Code and Codex" matrix
# (generated from bin/fleet-codex.sh's header — the single source of truth), so it
# is not restated here and cannot drift from it.
# ONE-TIME SETUP: Codex asks "Do you trust the contents of this directory?" once per
# project, and a worktree inherits its main repo's trust — so trust the BASE
# CHECKOUT once (`codex` in $FLEET_MAIN → 1/Yes). An untrusted base turns the first
# Codex pane red (`needs`) with the instruction in the pane, rather than stalling.
# @label=Worker agent CLI @group=spawn @tier=common @scope=fleet @edit=enum
#FLEET_AGENT="claude"

# Model a Codex worker launches with (`codex -m`). Codex model names are not
# Claude's, hence a separate key. Empty (default) defers to your own
# ~/.codex/config.toml `model`. Stamped on the window as @cc_model when set.
# @label=Codex worker model @group=spawn @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=str
#FLEET_CODEX_MODEL=""

# Optional native same-thread fallback for a model-specific quota cap. The
# account home must be registered for quota collection. No model-name guessing:
# explicitly map BOTH models to limit IDs reported by fleet-codex-model.sh limits.
# @label=Codex fallback model @group=spawn @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=str
#FLEET_CODEX_MODEL_FALLBACK=""
# @label=Codex model quota limit IDs @group=spawn @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=str
#FLEET_CODEX_MODEL_LIMIT_IDS='{}'

# Each Codex fleet pane gets a private local app-server for exact-session queue
# delivery. Its guardian shuts it down with the TUI, including a killed launcher.
# Set 0 only to troubleshoot the legacy embedded launch (no live queue delivery).
# @label=Codex private worker server @group=spawn @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=bool
#FLEET_CODEX_SERVER=1

# Check `codex --version` against the versions whose session (rollout) file
# format fleet's context% reader is verified on (0.154; issue #1079). A mismatch
# never blocks a launch: fleet-doctor shows a `codex` WARN and each Codex launch
# prints one warning. Set 0 once you have checked a newer Codex yourself.
# @label=Codex version check @group=spawn @tier=advanced @scope=global @edit=bool
#FLEET_CODEX_VERSION_CHECK=1

# Codex fleet commands launch as native `$fleet-*` skills when the selected
# CODEX_HOME has those skills installed and the Codex CLI advertises skills in
# `debug prompt-input`. Default `auto` keeps the old prose-expanded seed as the
# compatibility fallback; set 0/1 only for rollback or verification.
# @label=Codex native skills @group=spawn @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=enum @options=auto,0,1
#FLEET_CODEX_NATIVE_SKILLS=auto

# Optional account home for this Codex fleet. It must already exist and contain
# that account's native login/config; saved-session recovery retains its own home.
# @label=Codex account home @group=spawn @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=str
#FLEET_CODEX_HOME=""

# Space-separated labels registered with fleet-codex-account.sh register LABEL HOME.
# Fresh launches pick the most headroom; explicit/pinned/recovery homes win.
# @label=Codex account pool @group=spawn @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=str
#FLEET_CODEX_ACCOUNTS=""
# Hold fresh launches/autofill only when every configured home is known low.
# @label=Codex quota gate @group=spawn @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=bool
#FLEET_CODEX_QUOTA_GATE=0
# @label=Codex quota floor percent @group=spawn @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=int
#FLEET_CODEX_QUOTA_FLOOR=5
# @label=Codex quota cache seconds @group=spawn @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=int
#FLEET_CODEX_QUOTA_TTL=300
# Opt-in idle-only quota recovery through the durable handoff controller.
# @label=Codex quota migration @group=spawn @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=bool
#FLEET_CODEX_QUOTA_MIGRATE=0

# Codex MCP policy: unset inherits FLEET_MCP_CONFIG — so pointing that key at
# ~/.claude/fleet/conf/mcp-worker.json (recommended, issue #1078) also trims Codex
# workers to the same minimal set; explicitly empty preserves Codex defaults. `none` disables all configured MCP servers and apps/connectors.
# Otherwise use a JSON file/inline object with mcpServers (stdio/HTTP translation)
# or native mcp_servers; a native .toml file requires Python 3.11+. Strict policies
# disable unlisted servers, apps and automatic skill-driven MCP installation.
# Explicit caller -c overrides take precedence. No account config is rewritten.
# @label=Codex MCP policy @group=spawn @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=str
#FLEET_CODEX_MCP_CONFIG=""

# Defaults to a fleet-pinned Codex worker model; `inherit` or empty leaves native
# subagent selection alone. Claude aliases never cross over to Codex.
# @label=Codex subagent model @group=spawn @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=str
#FLEET_CODEX_SUBAGENT_MODEL=""
# @label=Codex subagent effort @group=spawn @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=str
#FLEET_CODEX_SUBAGENT_EFFORT=""

# Pre-answer Claude Code's "trust this folder?" dialog for THIS fleet's checkout at
# every spawn (issue #563). Claude Code keys trust on the resolved project root — a
# worktree resolves to its MAIN checkout — so when FLEET_MAIN is not trusted in
# ~/.claude.json every dispatched worker parks on the dialog with nobody to answer
# (macmini, 2026-09-12: 7+ min, slot "filled", nothing running). The launcher
# (bin/fleet-claude.sh → bin/fleet-trust.sh) writes
# projects[FLEET_MAIN].hasTrustDialogAccepted=true (and the worktree's) — SCOPED to
# the fleet's own checkout, atomic (temp + rename, race-checked against a claude
# process saving at the same instant), never a --dangerously-* flag. 0 turns it
# off; then trust the base once by hand (`claude` in $FLEET_MAIN → 1/Yes) or run
# `sh bin/fleet-trust.sh grant --main $FLEET_MAIN`. fleet-doctor.sh's `trust` line
# and fleet-up warn on an untrusted base; the dispatcher marks a parked pane `needs`.
# On a TRUSTED NODE (issue #2282 — `sh bin/fleet-trust.sh node`: the credential
# proxy's cached hub word, cred-proxy/trust.json, no older than a day, or the
# environment's FLEET_PRETRUST_NODE_MAX_AGE seconds) the launcher also pre-trusts every repo
# the fleet hosts and, for a fleet-opened no-repo session (@norepo 1 / the
# orchestrator) in $HOME, that one directory; untrusted / unknown = the narrow grant
# above, byte for byte. fleet-doctor's `信任框` row says which this machine is.
# @label=Pre-trust checkout at spawn @group=spawn @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=bool
#FLEET_PRETRUST=1

# The fleet mod (issue #1335, EPIC #1334): every Claude session the launcher
# (bin/fleet-claude.sh) opens loads mod/fleet/ with `--plugin-dir` — a Claude Code
# plugin that reports from inside the session. It checks the Claude Code version
# first (outside its supported range it registers nothing: window @mod_state
# off:version), then writes a heartbeat @mod_alive every 15s; scripts read it with
# fleet_mod_alive and take the mod's path only while it is fresh, today's path
# otherwise (which stays). fleet-doctor.sh's `mod` line counts alive / out of range
# / not loaded. 0 = no --plugin-dir at all: the launch argv is byte for byte what
# it was before the mod, and fleet_mod_alive is false everywhere. Login-wide.
# @label=Fleet mod in sessions @group=spawn @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=bool
#FLEET_MOD=1

# FLEET_STATUS_7501 — read the agent's own state report (issue #2536, EPIC #2535).
# Claude Code ≥ 2.1.295 says working / blocked / done as OSC 7501, but only to a
# terminal that answers its probe, and inside tmux nobody does — so fleet-claude.sh
# runs the agent under bin/fleet-status-7501.py's pty relay, which answers it and
# stamps @agent_status (+ @claude_state) off what it says. 0 = the agent runs bare,
# byte for byte as before (@agent_status never written). Login-wide; read at launch.
# @label=Agent's own state report (OSC 7501) @group=spawn @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=bool
#FLEET_STATUS_7501=1

# FLEET_STATUS_REPLAY_DEPTH — the agent's report on the person's own terminal too
# (issue #2539, EPIC #2535 C4): a terminal that knows OSC 7501 (Ghostty) shows a
# session's 在跑 / 需要你 + its words on the tab. tmux drops an OSC it does not know,
# so the relay re-sends each report as tmux passthrough, one copy per depth 1..N —
# the tmux servers between the agent and the terminal (Ghostty → node is 1; the
# fleet shell → stage → node is 3). 0 = the report stays on this machine. iTerm2
# has no OSC 7501 and ignores it. Login-wide; read at launch.
# @label=Agent state on your terminal: tmux depth @group=spawn @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int
#FLEET_STATUS_REPLAY_DEPTH=3

# A session's agent configuration, fixed at launch (issue #1782, EPIC #1776 C6):
# bin/fleet-claude.sh / fleet-codex.sh compose fleet default < team < local at
# every launch (bin/fleet-agent-team.py session), hand what the login's files lack
# (--settings / --mcp-config, Codex -c) and stamp the fingerprint as the window's
# @agent_cfg; $FLEET_CONF_DIR/global/agent-cfg.expected is what a fresh session
# would get. 0 = none of it: the launch argv is byte for byte what it was.
# @label=Fix agent config at launch @group=spawn @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=bool
#FLEET_AGENT_CFG=1

# The items fleet itself runs on (conf/agent-locked.list: the mod, the fleet
# hooks, the fleet's MCP servers) when this login overrides one: `warn` uses the
# login's value and lists it (launch stderr, fleet-doctor's `agentcfg` row);
# `enforce` ignores it and hands the fleet's. Everything else stays local-first.
# @label=Locked agent config @group=spawn @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=str
#FLEET_AGENT_LOCK=warn

# A session whose configuration fingerprint (@agent_cfg) is not the one a fresh
# session gets now is marked 配置旧 on the sidebar and counted by fleet-doctor's
# `agentcfg` row (issue #1783). `auto` reopens it — same conversation, new window
# (fleet-migrate.sh --cfg-stale) — once it has been idle (done, no /loop round, no
# background job) for FLEET_CFG_RESTART_IDLE seconds, at most
# FLEET_CFG_RESTART_MAX per fleet per tick; `ask` only raises an alert per session;
# `off` only marks it. A working / looping / waiting-on-you session is never touched.
# @label=Reopen stale-config sessions @group=spawn @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=str
#FLEET_CFG_RESTART=auto
# @label=Idle before a stale reopen @group=spawn @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_CFG_RESTART_IDLE=600
# @label=Stale reopens per fleet per tick @group=spawn @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int
#FLEET_CFG_RESTART_MAX=1

# --- Autofill dispatcher (bin/fleet-dispatch.sh, com.claude-fleet.dispatch) -----
# OFF by default. When enabled for a fleet, the dispatch daemon (~60s) keeps that
# fleet's worker slots filled FROM ITS BACKLOG, BY PRIORITY, whenever both caps
# above have headroom — auto-spawning the highest-priority ELIGIBLE issue so you
# don't hand-spawn every one.
#
# OPT-IN PER ISSUE (issue #421): an issue is only auto-spawned if it carries the
# canonical `autofill` label — you tag exactly the issues that may fill
# idle slots hands-off (like `autoland` opts a PR into hands-off land). So it is a
# TWO-key gate: this fleet armed (FLEET_AUTOFILL=1) AND the issue labelled. Full
# eligibility = open, unassigned, carries `autofill`, no live window bound, not
# labelled `blocked`. Priority = a `priority:p0|p1|p2` label tier
# (p0 first), then FIFO by issue number; unlabeled issues go last.
#
# COST: each auto-spawn launches a real Claude session that spends LLM tokens and
# opens a PR — it is aggressive, so it is opt-in per fleet. Set to 1 to enable
# (best per-fleet in $FLEET_CONF_DIR/<session>.conf). A fleet hosting several repos
# reads it PER REPO (issue #799): a repo's overlay may set 0 to opt that repo out,
# or 1 to arm just that repo; unset, the repo follows the fleet's value. The caps
# and FLEET_AUTOFILL_MAX_PER_TICK stay per fleet.
# @label=Auto-fill idle slots from labelled backlog @group=autofill @tier=common @scope=fleet @edit=bool
#FLEET_AUTOFILL=0
# Max issues auto-spawned per fleet per tick (rate-limit against a thundering
# herd; the ~60s interval is the cooldown between ticks). Default 1.
# @label=Auto-fill batch size @group=autofill @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=int @unit=per tick
#FLEET_AUTOFILL_MAX_PER_TICK=1
# Which machine auto-fill opens sessions on (issue #1425). Unset: the spawn's own
# default — FLEET_SPAWN_NODE (below; `auto` when the hub module is on,
# CCQUOTA_FLEET=1: the hub picks the least busy of your machines that host the
# repo), else this machine. `local` pins auto-fill here; a machine name pins it
# there.
# @label=Auto-fill machine @group=autofill @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=str
#FLEET_AUTOFILL_NODE=local
# The cross-machine hub, per fleet (issue #1539; docs/LOCAL-AND-HUB.md). `1`:
# this fleet's spawns take a hub lease and placement, its sidebar shows your
# sessions on the other machines, its status bar may switch to the hub layout,
# and the collector keeps the hub's session list fresh for it. `0`: this machine
# only — the one-machine fleet, byte for byte. Unset here: the login-wide value
# (an `export CCQUOTA_FLEET=1` in the install's fleet.conf or fleet.settings —
# the ccquota agent reads that same variable), so a login that never wrote this
# line behaves exactly as before. A fleet's line wins over the login's, both ways.
# @label=Cross-machine hub @group=accounts @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=bool
#CCQUOTA_FLEET=0
# Where EVERY new issue session in this fleet opens when nothing names a machine
# (issue #1475; a `--node` on the command, or FLEET_AUTOFILL_NODE for auto-fill,
# still wins). Only read with the hub module on (CCQUOTA_FLEET=1). `auto`: the hub
# picks among your machines that host the repo — and never one that is not READY
# (no gh login, no usable Claude/Codex credential, a checkout missing: its
# heartbeat says so); `local`: always this machine, the hub is not asked; a
# machine name: always there, even when it is not ready (you asked by name).
# Per fleet since #1539 (it was login-wide): a fleet's conf wins over the
# login's fleet.settings, which stays the default for every fleet.
# @label=New-session machine @group=accounts @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=str
#FLEET_SPAWN_NODE=auto

# --- Child → parent outcome reports (bin/fleet-report-parent.sh, issue #574) -----
# ON by default. When a worker that was SPAWNED BY ANOTHER SESSION (`@origin` names
# an `issue-<N>` / `scratch-<N>` window — a `fleet-issue-file.sh --spawn` follow-up,
# a scratch escalation) merges its PR, hits a blocker, or is reaped, it PUSHES a
# fixed four-line report to that session over the peer channel (fleet_peer_send, the
# SendMessage inbox socket — never send-keys). Before this, the parent's only option
# was to poll: the dash, `gh pr view`, ListAgents.
#
# COST, and why the switch exists: the report WAKES the parent for one turn, and the
# context it spends is context that session did not choose to spend. A fan-out of
# five children is five interrupts, and a parent near its handoff threshold can least
# afford them — which is why the envelope is capped at five lines and ends
# `no reply needed`. Hub-spawned work is unaffected either way (no parent ⇒ nothing
# is ever sent), so turn this off only if you fan out widely AND find the interrupts
# cost more than the polling they replace.
#
# TIERS (issue #938): not every report wakes the parent. Each is banded by
# report_tier (bin/fleet-children-lib.sh) and the band is written into the
# children ledger: loud (BLOCKED, FAILED not being fixed, REAPED unmerged/dirty, a
# true STOPPED, a child in `needs`) and quiet (MERGED, FAILED while fixing, other
# reaps) are delivered; silent (WAITING / IDLE — a child that ended its turn on an
# open PR or a background job) is ledger-only, never sent.
#   immediate (default; the legacy `1`) — loud + quiet delivered one by one.
#   batch     — the digest mode (issue #939): quiet reports are only recorded, and
#               the cleanup daemon's 60s tick (bin/fleet-children-flush.sh) delivers
#               them merged into ONE `[children-digest] 3/5 ✓ · 1 ⏳ · 1 !` — when a
#               loud report arrives (at once, carrying the queued quiet news), when
#               every child is terminal, when the parent is idle, or when the oldest
#               has waited FLEET_CHILD_REPORT_BATCH_SECS.
#   0         — off: nothing is sent and nothing is ledgered.
# @label=Child reports outcome to parent @group=spawn @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=enum
#FLEET_CHILD_REPORT=immediate

# How long a quiet child report may wait for its digest in FLEET_CHILD_REPORT=batch
# (issue #939), in seconds. The digest goes out earlier on a loud report, when every
# child is done, or when the parent is idle; this is the ceiling. Default 300.
# @label=Child-report digest max wait @group=spawn @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=int @unit=seconds
#FLEET_CHILD_REPORT_BATCH_SECS=300

# How long a child awaited by bin/fleet-await.sh may sit without progress before
# its STALL LADDER climbs a rung (issue #1268), in seconds: wake it, wake it again,
# re-send its task, tell its parent, then raise a `● #N · stalled` alert — one rung
# per this interval, each a `wake` row in the parent's children ledger. A new
# report or a change in the child's worktree resets it; an unknown state, or
# `working` with a busy process tree, never counts. 0 = off. Default 1200.
# @label=Stalled child escalation interval @group=spawn @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=int @unit=seconds
#FLEET_AWAIT_STALL_SECS=1200

# --- Default milestone for filed issues (bin/fleet-issue-file.sh, issue #433) ----
# Guarantee EVERY filed issue gets a milestone so nothing lands unsorted. When set,
# the one issue-filer channel (⌃n, the dash new-session box, the hub's file+spawn
# — all route through bin/fleet-issue-file.sh, #332) defaults a filing that passes no
# --milestone to THIS milestone; you re-bucket it to a proper theme during
# triage. The milestone is AUTO-CREATED if absent (idempotent), and it is BEST-EFFORT
# (issue #297): if the milestone plumbing fails the issue still files WITHOUT one, so
# ⌃n never fails to file. An explicit --milestone always wins. Empty/unset = OFF
# (current behaviour — filings stay milestone-less). Per-fleet: e.g. verkyyi/claude-fleet
# already has a `Triage` milestone; set it here to auto-sort every filing into Triage.
# @label=Default milestone for filed issues @group=backlog @tier=common @scope=fleet @edit=str
#FLEET_DEFAULT_MILESTONE=""

# --- Issue-bridge: issue comments → the bound worker's next turn (issue #132) ---
# OFF by default. When enabled for a fleet, the issue-bridge daemon
# (com.claude-fleet.issue-bridge, ~15s poll — or a webhook, see docs/ISSUE-BRIDGE.md)
# relays a trusted comment on a bound issue INTO that worker as its next turn.
# The issue thread becomes the single durable channel for driving a worker
# (operator→worker, worker→worker, external-collaborator→worker), replacing send-keys.
#
# SECURITY: a relayed comment becomes autonomous tool-use in a bypass-permissions
# worker — treat it as remote code execution. The author_association gate below is
# the headline control; un-gated relay on a PUBLIC repo is unsafe. Loop-safety: the
# bridge relays every comment from a trusted author UNLESS it carries the marker
# `<!-- fleet:no-relay -->` — fleet-internal comments (worker progress, an operator
# record) MUST be posted via bin/fleet-comment.sh (which stamps the marker), so
# nothing the fleet writes loops back in. See docs/ISSUE-BRIDGE.md.
# @label=Relay issue comments to worker @group=bridge @tier=common @scope=fleet @edit=bool
#FLEET_ISSUE_BRIDGE=0
# Trusted author_association floor (space-separated, verbatim GitHub values). Only
# comments whose author is in this set are relayed; NONE/CONTRIBUTOR are refused.
# Default: the three trusted tiers. Widen it ONLY if you understand the RCE risk.
# @label=Bridge: trusted associations @group=bridge @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=str
#FLEET_ISSUE_BRIDGE_ASSOC_FLOOR="OWNER MEMBER COLLABORATOR"
# Webhook HMAC secret (the `gh webhook forward` / cloudflared shared secret). Used
# by `--deliver` to validate that only GitHub triggered a delivery — MANDATORY for
# the webhook ingress (the poll ingress needs no secret). Keep it out of the conf
# in a real deploy; the daemon reads it from the environment. See docs.
# @label=Bridge: webhook HMAC secret @group=bridge @tier=advanced @scope=global @edit=str
#FLEET_ISSUE_BRIDGE_SECRET=""
# Revive a DORMANT issue: when a trusted comment lands on an OPEN issue whose
# worker window is gone, re-spawn the worker (its /fleet-claim re-reads the issue,
# comment and all). OFF by default — turn on once you trust the gate. A closed/
# landed issue is left alone (resume via #130 is a follow-up).
# @label=Bridge: revive dormant issues @group=bridge @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=bool
#FLEET_ISSUE_BRIDGE_REVIVE=0
# Max CONSECUTIVE typing-defers before a relay is delivered anyway (issue #195). The
# bridge DEFERS a relay while the target's ❯ input row holds un-submitted text (so it
# never prepends onto a human's half-typed line, issue #191). That defer is otherwise
# UNBOUNDED: a row that reads non-empty persistently (a stuck render, a future TUI
# placeholder, a capture-pane quirk) would wedge the channel forever, silently
# dropping a handback you are waiting on.
# After this many consecutive defers of the SAME comment, the bridge delivers anyway
# and logs a WARNING; the counter resets the moment the input clears, so a real
# partial is never clobbered. TRADE-OFF: keep it GENEROUS enough that a human pausing
# mid-type for a few minutes is respected, small enough that a bad read can't wedge.
# Default 20 ≈ 5 min at the 15s poll. Per-comment, persisted in the bridge state dir.
# @label=Bridge: max typing-defers @group=bridge @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=int @unit=ticks
#FLEET_BRIDGE_MAX_TYPING_DEFERS=20

# --- Cleanup daemon (bin/fleet-cleanup-daemon.sh, com.claude-fleet.cleanup) ------
# ON by default. THE FLEET NEVER MERGES — /fleet-ship arms GitHub auto-merge and
# GitHub (or a human on the web, or a collaborator) does the merge; the cleanup
# daemon (~60s) is what reaps the leftover worktree/window/branch and records the
# resume ledger once a PR is final (MERGED or CLOSED-unmerged). It merges NOTHING
# and relaxes NO approval gate, so it is safe to leave on for every fleet. Detection
# is cache + local (the prmap pr-refresh already writes + a local git/tmux check —
# ZERO extra gh); single-writer per repo + disk-gated. Set to 0 to opt a fleet OUT
# (the worktree-autoclean janitor still backstops merged worktrees). See docs/CLEANUP.md.
# @label=Cleanup daemon (reap after merges) @group=bridge @tier=common @scope=fleet @edit=bool
#FLEET_CLEANUP=1
# Wait after GitHub mergedAt before automatic cleanup (issue #565). Default 600s;
# 0 disables only this delay, not the existing cleanup gates. Missing/invalid or
# future merge timestamps defer when enabled. Manual fleet-cleanup.sh stays
# immediate; --auto --dry-run evaluates this gate without changing anything.
# Values outside 0..31536000 fall back to 600. This is not an idle-time policy.
# @label=Cleanup merged grace @group=bridge @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=int @unit=s
#FLEET_CLEANUP_MERGED_GRACE=600
# Seconds a CLOSED-unmerged PR's window must have been SILENT before cleanup may
# reap it (issue #544); the other half of that gate — "it has not spoken since the
# PR closed" — has no timeout. Also the restore picker's closed-PR cutoff. See
# docs/CLEANUP.md.
# @label=Cleanup closed grace @group=bridge @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=int @unit=s
#FLEET_CLEANUP_CLOSED_GRACE=900
# Reclaim clean raw/scratch windows that remain explicitly done this many minutes.
# The daemon records and verifies a resumable history entry first, and keeps the
# worktree, branch and transcript. 0 disables done-raw reaping. Default 30.
# Working/looping/pinned/active Fleet-loop sessions and unknown probes are kept.
# An unmerged PR requires strict ancestry; dirty worktrees are kept untouched.
# @label=Cleanup idle done raw sessions @group=bridge @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=int @unit=min
#FLEET_REAP_IDLE_DONE_MIN=30
# Close a finished session that no PR will ever close (issue #1832): an issue
# session whose issue is closed with no PR, or a spawned scratch with no policy,
# done this many seconds. History first; the worktree is dropped only when clean
# with nothing beyond the base, else only the window closes. Default 7200 (2h).
# @label=Cleanup finished sessions with no PR @group=bridge @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=int @unit=s
#FLEET_REAP_DONE_NO_PR_SECS=7200
# Preserve idle workers in their original windows; resume from the sleeping page
# (see FLEET_SLEEP_WAKE below) or on a due loop / quota / message. Start in
# observe mode (logs only); on supersedes the idle scratch-window disposal above.
# Unknown native idle/input/background-task evidence always keeps the agent alive.
# @label=Worker sleep @group=bridge @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=enum @options=off,observe,on
#FLEET_SLEEP=observe
# Continuous idle interval in seconds. Native completion/Stop evidence required;
# viewed workers, drafts, active jobs and ambiguous transfers prevent sleep.
# @label=Worker sleep idle grace @group=bridge @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=int @unit=s
#FLEET_SLEEP_AFTER=1800
# How a sleeping worker wakes when you go to it (issue #1050). confirm (default):
# arriving, attaching or leaving it on screen only shows its sleeping page; press
# its Wake button (⏎ or a tap) twice, or pick Wake in the sidebar row menu.
# dwell: the old behaviour — wake once the window has stayed current for 2s.
# Automatic wakes (due loop, quota back, incoming message) are unaffected.
# @label=Worker wake on arrival @group=bridge @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=enum @options=confirm,dwell
#FLEET_SLEEP_WAKE=confirm
# Seconds the sleeping page's Wake button stays armed after the first press;
# the second press must land inside it (and ≥0.3s after the first).
# @label=Worker wake confirm window @group=bridge @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=int @unit=s
#FLEET_SLEEP_WAKE_ARM=3
# Comma-separated native MCP server names whose volatile state may be discarded.
# Empty by default. Exact configured stdio launchers/package entrypoints only;
# child jobs/browser processes still prevent sleep. Do not list stateful REPLs.
# Applies to Claude and Codex workers; a Claude worker's set is the names in its
# FLEET_MCP_CONFIG (--mcp-config) document, e.g. mcp-image,playwright (#784). A
# worker without FLEET_MCP_CONFIG also runs the enabled plugins' servers, named
# plugin:<plugin>:<server>, e.g. plugin:playwright:playwright (#830).
# @label=Restartable MCP services @group=bridge @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=str
#FLEET_SLEEP_MCP_RESTARTABLE=
# Max PRs reaped per fleet per tick (a stampede guard; the ~60s interval is the
# cooldown). Default 4.
# @label=Cleanup batch size @group=bridge @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=int @unit=per tick
#FLEET_CLEANUP_MAX_PER_TICK=4
# Wall-clock budget for ONE candidate's fleet-cleanup.sh call (issue #587). The daemon
# is a single process on a ~60s interval, so a candidate that wedges (a hung `gh pr
# view`, `git pull`, or lease queue) stalls the cleanup of EVERY fleet behind it — one
# did, for 67 minutes. On expiry the whole process tree is TERMed then KILLed and the
# tick moves to the next candidate. A timeout spends a slot, so a tick is bounded by
# batch size x this. Default 120; 0 runs unbudgeted. See docs/CLEANUP.md.
# @label=Cleanup per-candidate timeout @group=bridge @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=int @unit=s
#FLEET_CLEANUP_CANDIDATE_TIMEOUT=120
# Also reap a MERGED PR whose head branch is NOT `issue-<N>` (issue #589). OFF by
# default, and the default is the historic behavior byte for byte. The whole reap
# chain is addressed by `issue-<N>`, so a session that started as a scratch
# (dash ⌃s → a `scratch-<N>` branch) and grew into a PR is NEVER cleaned up: its
# window hangs on the dash forever and its worktree keeps the disk (two were found
# at 1.2 GB and 5.4 GB, both long merged). That protection is deliberate (#543/#544)
# — a scratch window is routinely the OPERATOR's own workbench, so reaping on head
# branch alone would kill live work. Set to 1 and the reap is allowed, but only
# behind the worktree janitor's whole liveness gate PLUS "the window itself says it
# is finished": a worktree is checked out on the PR's head branch, that branch is
# not protected (FLEET_PROTECTED_RE) and is not the base checkout, its local tip is
# EXACTLY the commit GitHub merged (nothing pushed past the merge), the worktree is
# clean (untracked counts as dirty), and a live window's pane cwd sits inside the
# worktree and reports @claude_state=done — not working, not needs, not unset.
# The window lookup fails CLOSED: no window found means no reap (that worktree is
# worktree-autoclean.sh's case), so a missed lookup can never kill a live session.
# CLOSED-unmerged PRs are never included: that work never landed and stays put.
# Arm it per fleet once you trust the gate. See docs/CLEANUP.md.
# @label=Cleanup: reap non-issue (scratch) heads @group=bridge @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=bool
#FLEET_CLEANUP_SCRATCH_HEADS=0

# --- Close-on-exit hook (bin/session-end-hook.sh, SessionEnd) --------------------
# ON by default, GLOBALLY. The SessionEnd Claude Code hook reacts the instant an
# operator MANUALLY exits a worker (Ctrl-D / /exit, or logout): it closes the tmux
# window (no leftover shell), applies the SHARED reap gate (fleet_reap_ok) and acts on
# the worktree by verdict, and records the /fleet-history row NOW — so the session is
# indexed + resumable at once, not ~60s later when ledger-watch's snapshot-diff
# notices. The event-driven twin of FLEET_LEDGER_WATCH (issue #403), reusing the same
# shared reap primitives so it never diverges from the other reapers. Merged PR → reap
# worktree+branch, close the issue, `landed` row; ancestor-of-base → reap
# worktree+branch, `closed-unlanded` row (issue kept OPEN); committed-but-unmerged /
# dirty → KEEP the worktree + issue (resumable), `closed-unlanded` row. An @raw
# scratch is RECORDED into the ledger (keyed by its scratch-<N> slug, #466) and its
# window closed, but never reaped — disposal stays with ⌃x / the janitor. A /clear or
# /fleet-handoff cycle (reason=clear|resume) is a no-op, so it never fires on a
# handoff. Scoped to issue-bound workers (+ @raw scratch → window-close only); panels
# and the hub pane are never touched. Reacts, never blocks (SessionEnd can't veto).
#
# GLOBAL-authoritative off-switch: this knob is read machine-wide (snapshotted from
# THIS global fleet.conf before any per-fleet overlay). Set FLEET_CLOSE_ON_EXIT=0 here
# to disable it for the whole machine; a per-fleet conf value is IGNORED. Mirrors the
# default-on/opt-out idiom of FLEET_CLEANUP / FLEET_LEDGER_WATCH, but the switch is
# global-only, not per-fleet. See docs/CLEANUP.md.
# @label=Close window + reap on manual exit @group=bridge @tier=advanced @scope=global @edit=bool
#FLEET_CLOSE_ON_EXIT=1

# --- Webhook: fresh (~1s) PR/issue/CI status via `gh webhook forward` (issue #315) -
# OFF by default. When enabled for a fleet, the webhook daemon (bin/fleet-webhook.sh,
# com.claude-fleet.webhook — a KeepAlive supervisor like the spinner) makes GitHub's
# real-time webhook stream KICK the SAME single-writer refreshers the pollers use, so
# "CI went green" / "PR merged" / a new issue surfaces in ~1s instead of on the next
# poll tick. NO public endpoint: `gh webhook forward` (the cli/gh-webhook extension)
# registers the repo webhook against GitHub's OWN hosted relay and PULLS deliveries
# over the authenticated gh channel, re-POSTing each to a LOCALHOST handler — no
# tunnel, no exposed port. The handler only TRIGGERS a targeted pr-refresh/collector
# refresh (it never writes a cache itself), and polling stays the BACKSTOP, so a
# missed delivery costs only freshness, never correctness.
#
# PREREQ: `gh extension install cli/gh-webhook` (it registers a repo webhook — needs
# repo admin). One machine-wide daemon forwards one stream per opted-in repo of each
# LIVE fleet — EVERY repo the fleet hosts (issue #800); a repos/<slug>.conf may set
# its own FLEET_WEBHOOK to differ. Spends no LLM tokens, but keeps a `gh webhook
# forward` process per repo. See docs/WEBHOOK.md.
# @label=Fresh status via gh webhook forward @group=bridge @tier=common @scope=fleet @edit=bool
#FLEET_WEBHOOK=0
# The localhost port the handler binds (127.0.0.1 ONLY) and every forward re-POSTs to.
# GLOBAL: one handler serves every fleet. Change only on a port clash.
# @label=Webhook handler port @group=bridge @tier=advanced @scope=global @edit=int @unit=port
#FLEET_WEBHOOK_PORT=8917
# Optional HMAC shared secret. When set it is passed to `gh webhook forward --secret`
# and the handler verifies X-Hub-Signature-256 (reuses the issue-bridge --deliver
# check) — defense-in-depth for the relay→localhost hop. The handler already binds to
# localhost, so an unsigned delivery can at worst cause a spurious LOCAL refresh (never
# a cache write of its own), which is why this is optional. GLOBAL: one handler. Keep
# it out of the conf in a real deploy — the daemon reads it from the environment.
# @label=Webhook HMAC secret @group=bridge @tier=advanced @scope=global @edit=str
#FLEET_WEBHOOK_SECRET=""

# fleet-open's laptop half (issue #1380): the ssh alias, as seen from THIS machine,
# of the operator's computer running iTerm2. When set, fleet-doctor adds a `laptop`
# row — is extras/iterm2/fleet_open.py installed there, and the same version? A
# laptop that is asleep or off-network shows `?`, never a warning. Unset = no row.
# @label=Laptop ssh alias (fleet-open) @group=notify @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=str
#FLEET_OPEN_LAPTOP=""
# Advanced env-only knobs (NOT in this modal; override them in the environment): the
# forwarded event set in `FLEET_WEBHOOK_EVENTS` (default
# pull_request,check_run,check_suite,status,issues), the supervisor rescan cadence
# `FLEET_WEBHOOK_RESCAN` seconds (default 30), and the per-(event,repo) kick debounce
# `FLEET_WEBHOOK_DEBOUNCE` seconds that coalesces a CI event storm (default 3; 0
# disables). See docs/WEBHOOK.md.


# ── session lifecycle emitter (issue #625) ────────────────────────────────────
# OFF unless FLEET_EMIT_URL is set — and OFF means OFF: no spool file, no socket,
# no behaviour change anywhere. The fleet's value is that it works on a laptop with
# nothing else installed, and that stays true.
#
# WHAT IT IS FOR. The fleet is the only component that knows what a session was
# FOR: it binds a session to an issue, gives it a worktree, and watches the PR that
# comes out of it. A usage ledger downstream knows what each session COST, keyed by
# session id, with no idea which issue was worked — so you can see that a week
# burned N tokens and, separately, that M issues closed, and you cannot connect
# them. This emitter is the one field that joins the two. It is an emitter ONLY:
# the fleet stays a tmux product, and the cross-session, over-time view (spend
# against outcome, backlog flow) is a different product's job.
#
# WHAT IS SENT — the whole list: event name, timestamp, the fleet's session name,
# the Claude session id, repo, issue number, PR number, branch, and the outcome
# words (source/reason/state/action/outcome/verdict). NO prompt or transcript
# content, no file paths, no issue or window titles, no hostname or username, no
# credentials. Four events: session.start, session.bind, session.pr, session.end.
# Fire-and-forget over a bounded spool — a dead endpoint is a silent no-op, never a
# stalled worker. See docs/EMIT.md, which lists every field of every event.
#
# PER-FLEET: two fleets watching different repos can report to different endpoints.
# @label=Session lifecycle endpoint @group=bridge @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=str
#FLEET_EMIT_URL=""
# Bearer token sent as `Authorization: Bearer <token>`. Identity rides HERE, which
# is why the payload carries no hostname or user. Keep it out of the conf in a real
# deploy — every emit path reads it from the environment just as happily.
# @label=Session lifecycle token @group=bridge @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=str
#FLEET_EMIT_TOKEN=""
# Advanced env-only knobs (NOT in this modal): `FLEET_EMIT_QUEUE_MAX`, the spool cap
# in events (default 500 — on overflow the OLDEST are dropped, so a permanently
# dead endpoint costs bounded disk and keeps the fresh tail), and
# `FLEET_EMIT_TIMEOUT`, the per-POST curl timeout in seconds (default 5).


# Seconds the backlog panel's issue preview (⌃p) caches each issue's detail
# before re-fetching from gh. Lower = fresher body/comments, more API chatter.
# GLOBAL: read by the preview tool from the global env — a per-fleet override
# would be a silent no-op, so this is machine-wide.
# @label=Issue cache lifetime @group=cache @tier=advanced @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_ISSUE_TTL=180

# Seconds the collector caches gh fetches (open issues + the repo-wide PR map)
# before re-fetching. Lower = fresher backlog/PR data, more gh API chatter.
# GLOBAL: the collector is ONE daemon serving every fleet and sets this once —
# it is not per-fleet overridable.
# @label=GitHub cache lifetime @group=cache @tier=common @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_GH_TTL=90

# One account, one set of background reads (issue #1271). Several logins on one
# machine that share a GitHub token each ran the same collector / pr-refresh /
# issue-bridge reads. With 1, they elect ONE leader (oldest live heartbeat in
# /Users/Shared/.fleet-gh/<token-fp>/, taken over within 3 collector ticks when it
# stops): only the leader asks GitHub, the others read its fresh copy, and a repo
# the leader does not poll is still fetched by whoever needs it. `fleet-doctor`'s
# github row names this login's role. Set it on EVERY login that shares the token.
# GLOBAL: the daemons are machine-wide, so it is not per-fleet overridable.
# @label=Share background reads across logins @group=cache @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=bool
#FLEET_GH_SHARE=0

# Collector tick deadline (issue #551). ONE collector tick runs at a time
# (global/collect.pid): a tick started while the previous one is still running
# skips — unless the previous one is older than this, in which case it is
# treated as wedged (an un-timeboxed `gh` on a bad network held a tick for
# hours), its whole process tree is TERMed and the new tick takes over. A
# healthy 26-worktree tick takes ~20s since #552 budgeted the git phase (it
# used to run 1–3 min); keep this well above whatever yours does. The
# per-phase seconds of the last tick are in global/collect.heartbeat
# (fleet-doctor prints the slowest phase). GLOBAL: one daemon, one deadline.
# @label=Collector tick deadline @group=cache @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_COLLECT_DEADLINE=600

# Collector GIT-PHASE budget (issue #552). The git phase reads each live
# worktree's branch (+ahead/-behind) into global/git_<key>. It used to also run
# `git status --porcelain` per worktree — a full working-tree scan measured at
# 4m42s on one 24haowan-monorepo worktree, feeding a dirty flag NO reader ever
# consumed — which pushed the whole tick past its own 60s StartInterval. launchd
# does not overlap a StartInterval job, so the collector's real cadence decayed
# to "one tick's duration" (5.3 min observed) and every cache went stale. The
# status call is gone; this is the rail that keeps the phase bounded anyway. On
# expiry the phase's process tree is killed, the tick RUNS ON (the remaining
# worktrees keep last tick's branch) and the blown budget is logged. A healthy
# 26-worktree phase is ~2s, so the default is ~15x headroom. GLOBAL.
# @label=Collector git budget @group=cache @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_COLLECT_GIT_BUDGET=30

# Name a SLOW worktree on stderr (logs/collect.launchd.log) when its git work
# takes at least this long. The heartbeat only carries the phase total, so
# without this a phase creeping toward the budget above says nothing about WHICH
# worktree is dragging it. GLOBAL.
# @label=Collector git slow-log @group=cache @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_COLLECT_GIT_SLOW=10

# Collector WHOLE-TICK budget (issue #653) — the backstop over every phase.
# Budgeting phases one at a time is a losing game: whichever is currently the
# most expensive drags the tick past its own 60s StartInterval, and since launchd
# never overlaps a StartInterval job, "tick duration" IS the collector's real
# cadence. #552 budgeted `git` (571s → 56s) and the very next tick measured
# `dur=454 … git=126 usage=226` — the bottleneck had simply moved to a phase with
# no budget, and `runs` advanced once in 514s against a 60s interval. So every
# phase now has its own budget (the knobs below) and this one bounds their sum:
# before each phase the remaining time is checked and that phase's budget is
# CLAMPED to what is left, so the tick cannot overrun by more than its last
# phase's tail. Truncation is not starvation — the phase the tick stopped at is
# parked in global/collect.phase.cursor and the NEXT tick starts there, wrapping
# round, so a truncated phase waits one round rather than forever. A tick that
# completes clears the cursor, so a healthy machine (a whole tick is ~10–20s)
# never rotates and this is invisible.
#
# What this buys, measured (6 consecutive live ticks, #653): launchd restarts the
# job a constant ~60s after the previous tick ENDS, so the collector's real cadence
# is simply
#
#     cycle = tick duration + 60s
#
#   dur  67s -> 128s cycle      dur 143s -> 204s cycle
#   dur  77s -> 138s cycle      dur 184s -> 245s cycle
#
# (fits every sample within 3s). So bounding the tick pays back LINEARLY — there is
# no threshold to clear, and the 454s tick in #653 was costing a ~514s cycle, which
# is exactly the "runs advanced once in 514s" that opened the issue.
#
# Why 2x the interval and not less: a smaller budget buys cadence by TRUNCATING, and
# a truncated phase then waits a whole rotation. At 120s a tick runs every phase, so
# every cache is at most one cycle old; at, say, 50s the cycle would be ~110s but
# each cache would only refresh every 3-4 ticks — staler in absolute terms than the
# 120s setting despite the better `runs` count. `runs` is a proxy for freshness, not
# freshness itself, and below roughly one full tick's work the two pull apart.
# Why not more: the dash's staleness alarm fires when the heartbeat has not advanced
# for FLEET_DAEMON_STALE_MULT x 60s (300s by default), so past ~250s the collector
# starts tripping its own alarm — which is what the 454s ticks were doing. Raise the
# offending phase's budget instead, or find out why it got slow. GLOBAL.
# @label=Collector tick budget @group=cache @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_COLLECT_TICK_BUDGET=120

# Per-phase collector budgets (issue #653). One knob each, so a phase that is
# legitimately slow on your machine can be given room without loosening the rest;
# FLEET_COLLECT_TICK_BUDGET above is the backstop over all of them. On expiry the
# phase's process tree is killed, the tick RUNS ON, and the blown budget is named
# on stderr (logs/collect.launchd.log) — `fleet-doctor.sh` also reports it. The
# git phase keeps its historical name, FLEET_COLLECT_GIT_BUDGET above.
# 0 runs that phase UNBUDGETED (the convention every budget knob here shares): it
# still needs room in the tick to start, but once started nothing cuts it short —
# not even FLEET_COLLECT_TICK_BUDGET. That is for a fake-backed selftest or a
# one-off diagnosis, not for a phase that is slow: raise its budget instead.
#   quotawatch  the ccquota pre-emptive rotation, run first (it has its own 60s
#               daemon too, so this in-tick run is a backstop)
#   sockets     enumerating the live fleet sockets — the tick's one precondition
#   guide       first-login guide recovery (only when a first attempt is pending)
#   sessmap     session→repo resolution + the repo fetch queue
#   issues      the per-repo `gh issue list` fetches (TTL-gated; network)
#   ctx         per-worktree context tokens (python over the newest transcript)
#   usage       the 5h/7d token proxy (python over ALL transcripts; TTL ≥300s).
#               This was the tick's biggest unbudgeted block: 226s measured
#               against 0.2s of the same work at foreground priority
#   scrape      capture-pane sweep for the official rate-limit line
#   banner      multi-account limit banner → mark-limited + rotate
#   escalate    detached-attention notifications
#   snapshot    the crash-recovery layout snapshot
#   hubsess     keeps the other machines' sessions refresh loop running
#               (fleet-hub-sessions.sh --ensure; only with CCQUOTA_FLEET=1, #1423)
# @label=Collector quotawatch budget @group=cache @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_COLLECT_QUOTAWATCH_BUDGET=30
# @label=Collector sockets budget @group=cache @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_COLLECT_SOCKETS_BUDGET=20
# @label=Collector guide budget @group=cache @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_COLLECT_GUIDE_BUDGET=30
# @label=Collector sessmap budget @group=cache @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_COLLECT_SESSMAP_BUDGET=30
# @label=Collector issues budget @group=cache @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_COLLECT_ISSUES_BUDGET=45
# @label=Collector ctx budget @group=cache @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_COLLECT_CTX_BUDGET=45
# @label=Collector usage budget @group=cache @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_COLLECT_USAGE_BUDGET=60
# @label=Collector scrape budget @group=cache @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_COLLECT_SCRAPE_BUDGET=30
# @label=Collector banner budget @group=cache @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_COLLECT_BANNER_BUDGET=30
# The banner phase scans every window each tick, so its budget is
# max(FLEET_COLLECT_BANNER_BUDGET, windows × this) — the budget above is the floor (issue #1588).
# @label=Collector banner per-window budget @group=cache @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=ms
#FLEET_COLLECT_BANNER_PER_WINDOW_MS=1500
# @label=Collector escalate budget @group=cache @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_COLLECT_ESCALATE_BUDGET=30
# The agentcfg phase (issue #2076): every stale / renew window's start judged against
# the live install (fleet-oldcfg-check.sh --sweep) — the sidebar's red 会坏·需重开.
# @label=Collector agentcfg budget @group=cache @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_COLLECT_AGENTCFG_BUDGET=20
# @label=Collector snapshot budget @group=cache @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_COLLECT_SNAPSHOT_BUDGET=30
# @label=Collector hubsess budget @group=cache @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_COLLECT_HUBSESS_BUDGET=10

# Collector STALENESS threshold (issue #636) — when the dash starts saying so.
# The dash draws every number out of the collector's caches, so a collector that
# stops does not blank the dash: it freezes it, with nothing to show the data is
# old (2026-09-14: launchd PENDED com.claude-fleet.collect for 103 minutes,
# `last exit code = 0`, and the dash showed a two-hour-old world). Once
# global/collect.heartbeat has not advanced for this long the status bar shows
# `⚠ dash stale 47m`.
#
# AN ABSOLUTE OVERRIDE, and normally leave it unset (issue #639). It used to
# default to FLEET_COLLECT_DEADLINE (600s) on the reasoning that a tick past the
# deadline is killed, so nothing legitimate could be older — true, and blind to
# the failure that actually happens: launchd running this 60s unit once per 7–14
# MINUTES. Every number on the dash twelve times too old, and the heartbeat age
# never crossing 600s, so the verdict read `fresh` for hours. Unset, the threshold
# is FLEET_DAEMON_STALE_MULT × this unit's own 60s interval (300s), which catches
# the degradation and the full stop with one number. A long-but-WORKING tick is
# not affected: an in-flight tick (global/collect.pid, younger than
# FLEET_COLLECT_DEADLINE above) counts as evidence of life on its own, so a fleet
# whose single git phase runs for minutes — 551s is on record from a monorepo —
# still never trips the alarm. GLOBAL: one collector, one threshold.
# @label=Dash staleness alarm @group=cache @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_COLLECT_STALE=600

# Collector SELF-HEAL (issue #636). `launchctl kickstart -k` unsticks a pended
# collector instantly, so whoever notices the staleness above kicks the daemon:
# the status bar (while attached) and the quotawatch daemon (headless). Set 0 to
# keep the alarm but never kick.
# @label=Dash self-heal @group=cache @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=bool
#FLEET_COLLECT_KICK=1

# Minimum seconds between two self-heal kicks. This is the rail that keeps a
# permanently broken daemon from being kicked once per status-bar render (every
# 5s, per attached client): the kick claims the stamp atomically and one kick
# costs the whole cooldown even if it fails. Every kick is logged to
# logs/collect-kick.log. GLOBAL.
# @label=Self-heal cooldown @group=cache @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_COLLECT_KICK_COOLDOWN=600

# How long the status bar keeps the self-heal TRACE up (`↻ dash kicked 4m`) after
# the collector recovers. A silent recovery would hide the very outage this
# exists to surface, so the trace outlives the alarm by design. GLOBAL.
# @label=Self-heal trace window @group=cache @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_COLLECT_KICK_TRACE=1800

# ---- interval-daemon liveness: EVERY unit, not just the collector (#639) ----
# #636 instrumented the collector. Two days later the same machine showed the
# fault is domain-wide: launchd stopped scheduling every StartInterval unit at
# once, all of their logs freezing inside the same two minutes, while the two
# KeepAlive units never missed a frame. Measured over 27.8 minutes by `runs`:
# collect got 3 of ~28 due, and cleanup / dispatch / base-sync / issue-bridge /
# ledger-watch / quotawatch got ZERO — each having last run at a hand-kick
# minutes earlier. So cleanup stops reaping workers, dispatch stops autofilling,
# base-sync stops fast-forwarding the base, issue-bridge stops relaying comments
# (`fleet-comment.sh --to-worker` silently reaches nobody), ledger-watch stops
# indexing closed sessions — none of it visible, because nothing errors.
#
# bin/fleet-daemon-watch.sh watches all of them off a per-unit scheduling stamp,
# alarms on the status bar (`⚠ daemon stale cleanup,dispatch+2`) and in
# fleet-doctor, and self-heals — driven by the KeepAlive spinner, because an
# interval unit is being pended right alongside its patient.

# Staleness MULTIPLIER: a unit is stale once its last tick is this many times its
# own StartInterval old. Relative, because the units differ by 240× (15s for
# issue-bridge, 3600s for worktree-autoclean) and because DEGRADATION — not a
# clean stop — is the usual shape, which no single absolute number can see. 5 is
# well clear of one slow tick yet far below the 7–14-minute cadence #639
# measured; raise it on a fleet whose ticks are genuinely long. GLOBAL.
# @label=Daemon staleness multiplier @group=cache @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int
#FLEET_DAEMON_STALE_MULT=5

# Floor under that multiple, so the 15s units (issue-bridge, pr-refresh) do not
# alarm on one slow `gh` round-trip. GLOBAL.
# @label=Daemon staleness floor @group=cache @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_DAEMON_STALE_FLOOR=180

# Idle gate (issue #1077): once this login has had NO live fleet for this many
# seconds, collect and quotawatch do a full tick only once per this many seconds
# (≤ 12 an hour at 300, instead of 60), and the spinner naps 60s instead of 2s.
# Any live fleet socket, or a spawn (fleet-up, the dash, dispatch, cw), wakes all
# three at once. launchd's `runs =` still counts every start — a gated tick exits
# after one stamp. 0 = off: every tick works, as before #1077. GLOBAL.
# @label=Idle daemon gate @group=cache @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_DAEMON_IDLE_AFTER=300

# Per-unit ABSOLUTE override, in place of the multiple: add a line of the form
# FLEET_DAEMON_STALE_<UNIT>=<seconds>, the unit name uppercased with `-` → `_`
# (so `base-sync` is FLEET_DAEMON_STALE_BASE_SYNC). Use it for a unit whose real
# cadence is not its StartInterval; FLEET_COLLECT_STALE above is the same thing
# for `collect`. Deliberately not listed as a key here — the set is per unit, so
# the config modal would show ten arbitrary rows nobody edits. The same shape
# exists for the kick cooldown (FLEET_DAEMON_KICK_COOLDOWN_<UNIT>) and for a host
# that templated a different cadence (FLEET_DAEMON_INTERVAL_<UNIT>).

# Daemon SELF-HEAL master switch. 0 keeps every alarm and kicks nothing — the
# right setting while you are diagnosing WHY a unit stalls. GLOBAL.
# @label=Daemon self-heal @group=cache @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=bool
#FLEET_DAEMON_KICK=1

# Minimum seconds between two kicks OF THE SAME UNIT, as a MULTIPLE of that
# unit's own StartInterval. Per unit, so a second stalled daemon is still helped
# while the first is cooling down. Every kick, and every refusal to kick, is
# logged to logs/daemon-kick.log.
#
# It scales, rather than being a flat number, because of what a kick becomes when
# the whole launchd domain stops spawning (#711): `launchctl kickstart` is an
# EXPLICIT command, so it still works when nothing is being scheduled — and the
# cooldown then silently IS every daemon's period. The flat 600s this used to be
# was sized for the 60s units, and on 2026-09-15 it turned issue-bridge and
# pr-refresh (StartInterval=15) into ten-minute daemons: a `--to-worker` relay
# that should land in 15s took up to ten minutes, and the 60s quota watch #551
# built after an 85% window was missed entirely became one look per ten minutes.
# 3 keeps the cooldown at or below every unit's staleness threshold, which hands
# the rate-limiting back to that already-per-unit number. GLOBAL.
# @label=Daemon self-heal cooldown multiplier @group=cache @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int
#FLEET_DAEMON_KICK_COOLDOWN_MULT=3

# Floor under that multiple, so the 15s units do not get kicked every few seconds
# while the domain is down. GLOBAL.
# @label=Daemon self-heal cooldown floor @group=cache @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_DAEMON_KICK_COOLDOWN_FLOOR=60

# ABSOLUTE cooldown, in place of the multiple — one number for every unit, which
# is what this knob meant before #711. Unset by default. GLOBAL.
# @label=Daemon self-heal cooldown (absolute) @group=cache @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_DAEMON_KICK_COOLDOWN=600

# How long the status bar keeps the aggregate trace up (`↻ daemon kicked 3m`)
# after the units recover. GLOBAL.
# @label=Daemon trace window @group=cache @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_DAEMON_KICK_TRACE=1800

# Ineffective kicks before ESCALATING to a real unload + reload of the unit
# (`launchctl bootout` + `bootstrap`). A kickstart buys ONE execution, not
# restored scheduling — that is the whole lesson of the `runs` table above, where
# every dead unit had been hand-kicked minutes before and never ran again. The
# counter resets the moment the unit ticks. 0 disables the escalation and leaves
# kickstart as the only remedy. GLOBAL.
# @label=Daemon reload after N kicks @group=cache @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int
#FLEET_DAEMON_RELOAD_AFTER=3

# Minimum seconds between two reloads of the same unit — much longer than the
# kick cooldown, because a reload takes the unit out of the domain and puts it
# back. A reload that leaves it UNLOADED does NOT consume this: the next pass
# bootstraps it again at once, since an unloaded unit is worse than a pended one.
# GLOBAL.
# @label=Daemon reload cooldown @group=cache @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_DAEMON_RELOAD_COOLDOWN=1800

# --- the launchd DOMAIN probe (issue #711) -------------------------------------
# Every knob above answers "is THIS unit being scheduled?". These answer the
# question one level up, and they exist because on 2026-09-15 the honest per-unit
# answer was wrong nine times over: the whole `gui/501` domain had stopped
# spawning jobs, so fleet-doctor printed nine "nothing is scheduling
# com.claude-fleet.<x>" lines and sent the operator to read plists that were
# fine. bin/fleet-launchd-probe.sh settles it by bootstrapping a throwaway agent
# that shares nothing with the fleet and counting how often launchd runs it; when
# several units stall together, fleet-doctor asks it and collapses the nine lines
# into one that says "machine, not fleet — log out or reboot".

# Master switch for the probe. 0 keeps every per-unit line and never measures the
# domain — which also means never ruling the machine out. GLOBAL.
# @label=launchd domain probe @group=cache @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=bool
#FLEET_LAUNCHD_PROBE=1

# How long the probe watches its throwaway agent. This is WALL CLOCK inside a
# fleet-doctor run, so it is the one number here with a felt cost; it must stay
# comfortably above two StartIntervals or a healthy domain can read as degraded.
# GLOBAL.
# @label=launchd probe window @group=cache @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_LAUNCHD_PROBE_WINDOW=40

# The throwaway agent's own StartInterval. Floored at 10s in the script: launchd
# throttles a job to one spawn per 10s, so anything smaller measures the throttle
# instead of the domain. GLOBAL.
# @label=launchd probe interval @group=cache @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_LAUNCHD_PROBE_INTERVAL=15

# How long a verdict is reused before it is measured again, so repeated doctor
# runs during one incident pay the window once. GLOBAL.
# @label=launchd probe verdict TTL @group=cache @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_LAUNCHD_PROBE_TTL=900

# How many interval units must be stalled AT ONCE before the stall counts as
# domain-shaped and the probe is worth running (it must also be at least half of
# the units that have ever ticked on this host). Below this, a stalled unit is
# treated as its own problem — which it usually is. GLOBAL.
# @label=Domain-stall unit threshold @group=cache @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int
#FLEET_DAEMON_DOMAIN_MIN=3

# …or that many units KICKED inside this window. The second signature, and the
# one that matters once the self-heal works: a kick buys one execution, so a
# kicked unit reads fresh again for a whole interval and "how many are overdue
# right now" collapses to one or two while every daemon on the box is really
# running at its cooldown. On a healthy machine this count is 0 — a kick only ever
# happens because something stopped being scheduled. GLOBAL.
# @label=Domain-stall kick window @group=cache @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_DAEMON_DOMAIN_KICK_WINDOW=3600

# PR-status refresh cadence (bin/tmux-pr-refresh.sh, com.claude-fleet.pr-refresh).
# PR/CI state (the dash PR cell + the status-bar @prci glyph) is refreshed by its
# OWN fast daemon, decoupled from the 60s collector, so "CI went green" / "PR
# merged" surfaces within ~this many seconds — which is when you are
# watching to land it. Default 15. This value gates the script's own gh-fetch
# dedup; the DAEMON fire cadence is set in the launchd plist (StartInterval) /
# systemd timer (OnUnitActiveSec) — keep all three in step if you change it.
# GLOBAL: one daemon serves every fleet and its cadence is tied to the plist —
# not per-fleet overridable.
# @label=PR status refresh @group=cache @tier=common @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_PR_REFRESH_INTERVAL=15

# Poll backoff (issue #892): the two ~15s GitHub pollers — pr-refresh and the
# issue-bridge poll — slow down per repo while nothing changes. A poll that comes
# back identical doubles that repo's wait (15 → 30 → 60), capped at this many
# seconds; any change, a webhook kick or a bridge delivery drops it straight back
# to 15. 60 is the recommended value: a merged PR still reaches the dash within a
# minute (at once with the webhook installed). Default 15 = no backoff, as before.
# GLOBAL: one daemon of each serves every fleet.
# @label=Poll backoff ceiling @group=cache @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_POLL_MAX_BACKOFF=15

# --- "merged" ≠ "live": deploy state for merged PRs (issue #541) ------------------
# Once a PR is MERGED the dash PR cell reads `merged`. With one of the two knobs
# below set for THIS fleet, bin/tmux-pr-refresh.sh also probes where the merge
# commit actually is and the cell becomes `live` (green) / `deploy…` (still
# rolling out) / `deploy✗` (a post-merge run went red); the ⌃t landed list shows
# the same in its `dep` column. Per-fleet: each repo deploys its own way.
#
# A) A local git checkout that IS the deployment — the merge sha is live once it is
# an ancestor of that checkout's HEAD. Zero network. For claude-fleet itself this
# is the live install `$HOME/.claude/fleet`, which /fleet-sync-install
# fast-forwards. Wins over the Actions check below when both are set.
# @label=Deploy ref (local checkout) @group=cache @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=str
#FLEET_DEPLOY_REF=""

# B) GitHub Actions: the merge sha's post-merge workflow runs (the push-to-master
# runs plus any workflow_dispatch deploys a conductor fans out with the same
# head_sha). All green → live; any still running → deploying; any red → failed;
# none → unknown. A sha that reached `live` is never re-read; the others are
# looked at again after FLEET_DEPLOY_TTL seconds (default 60), a `failed` one
# only after FLEET_DEPLOY_TTL_FAILED (900). When two or more are due, ONE listing
# of the base branch's newest runs answers all of them; shas older than that
# window take a per-sha `gh api`, at most FLEET_DEPLOY_PROBE_MAX (4) a tick
# (claude-fleet#1211). Only `actions` is understood.
# @label=Deploy check (Actions runs) @group=cache @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=str
#FLEET_DEPLOY_CHECK=""

# Stuck-working demotion (bin/tmux-spinner.sh). A window whose Stop hook was
# missed (crash/race/a turn that didn't emit Stop) stays pinned at
# @claude_state=working forever — the classifier backstop skips working windows.
# The spinner catches it marker-agnostically: a live Claude session repaints its
# pane at least once/second (the elapsed-time counter ticks) so tmux's
# window_activity stays fresh, while a stopped session's pane freezes. A working
# window idle (window_activity stale) longer than this many seconds is demoted to
# done and reclassified. Conservative by design — a live worker never exceeds ~1s
# of activity age (validated), so 120 is a ~120× margin; bias is toward leaving a
# stuck window a tick longer, never toward demoting a busy one. GLOBAL: one spinner
# daemon serves every fleet.
#
# ⚠️ This is also the FLOOR under auto-handoff (issue #677): /fleet-handoff's cycle
# waits for `working` to clear, and on a turn that never emitted Stop (a model cap,
# #580) this demotion is the only thing that ever clears it — so
# FLEET_STUCK_WORKING_SECS + 20s must stay well below FLEET_HANDOFF_IDLE_TIMEOUT
# (above), or auto-handoff silently stops working. Setting it to 0 DISABLES the
# demoter, which disables auto-handoff along with it — fleet-doctor.sh says so.
# @label=Demote stuck-working after @group=status @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_STUCK_WORKING_SECS=120
# Native-truth reconcile (issue #806): a `working` window whose Claude session
# registry (~/.claude/sessions/<pid>.json) or bound Codex thread reports idle for
# at least this long, while the working stamp is equally old, is demoted to done and
# re-classified — the floor the activity demoter above cannot provide for an idle
# TUI that keeps repainting its footer. Runs first on the sleep daemon's 60s tick,
# independent of the spinner. 0 disables the pass.
# @label=Reconcile native-idle working after @group=status @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_STATE_IDLE_SECS=30
# Degenerate-output watchdog (issue #1557): a `working` window whose screen is
# >= FLEET_DEGENERATE_LINES non-blank rows of one short unit repeated (`<br>` ×16,822
# on 2026-10-04) on two consecutive spinner sweeps gets ONE Escape, @degenerate_ts,
# a DEGENERATE row in its parent's child ledger and a `⟲` at the end of its sidebar
# row for FLEET_DEGENERATE_MARK_SECS. At most one Escape per window per
# FLEET_DEGENERATE_COOLDOWN_SECS. Full screen → Escape: ~2 × the sweep. 0 disables.
# @label=Degenerate-output sweep @group=status @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_DEGENERATE_SECS=15
# @label=Degenerate-output row floor @group=status @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int
#FLEET_DEGENERATE_LINES=12
# @label=Degenerate-output Escape cooldown @group=status @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_DEGENERATE_COOLDOWN_SECS=300
# @label=Degenerate-output ⟲ mark @group=status @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_DEGENERATE_MARK_SECS=1800

# Show a compact live hub list on the left of worker/scratch windows. prefix+e
# toggles and saves this preference for the fleet; prefix+E browses by keyboard.
# Automatically hidden below sidebar width + 81 columns, reserving 80 for work.
# Only the visible window owns a view; background windows do not repaint it.
# @label=Worker task sidebar @group=status @tier=common @scope=fleet @edit=bool
#FLEET_SIDEBAR=1

# Language for tmux UI labels and fleet-facing toasts. `auto` follows this
# login's locale (zh* => Chinese, en* => English; C/unset keeps existing Chinese);
# set en or zh to force.
# @label=Tmux UI language @group=status @tier=common @scope=fleet @edit=enum @options=auto,en,zh
#FLEET_UI_LANG=auto

# Sidebar width in terminal cells (clamped to 24–60) — the FLOOR since issue
# #1328: the sidebar widens itself to fit its longest row, up to
# FLEET_SIDEBAR_WIDTH_MAX, never past a quarter of the window nor into the
# worker's 80 columns. Drag it by hand and it keeps your width from then on
# (the session's @sidebar_width_manual; unset it to go back to auto). Either
# width is HELD (issue #1521): a window that takes a narrower client's size
# scales every pane, and the sidebar snaps back rather than staying scaled.
# Narrow screens hide the sidebar without changing the saved on/off preference.
# @label=Task sidebar width @group=status @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=int @unit=columns
#FLEET_SIDEBAR_WIDTH=30

# The widest the sidebar grows on its own to fit a long name (issue #1328).
# Set it to FLEET_SIDEBAR_WIDTH to turn the auto-width off.
# @label=Task sidebar max auto width @group=status @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=columns
#FLEET_SIDEBAR_WIDTH_MAX=44

# What the task list shows by default (issue #2675): `batch` — each batch one
# row with its sessions folded under it (a session waiting on you gives the
# batch a `!`, never a red row of its own), each repo's sessions with no batch
# one 「单独的活 · N」 row, folded until you open it; 停放 / 待你动手 rows when the
# steward reports them. `off` — every session its own row, as before.
# @label=Task list folds into batches @group=status @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=str
#FLEET_SIDEBAR_FOLD=batch

# The client's macOS notification when a session newly waits on you (issue
# #1951): the title says who, the body what it asks, a click goes there. Once per
# wait (deduped by the alert id); 0 = no notification (the bar's 「! n 等你」 stays).
# @label=Notify when a session asks you @group=status @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=bool
#FLEET_NOTIFY=1

# A notification whose notifier cannot run a click (osascript, iTerm2's OSC 9)
# only brings the terminal forward; that focus-in jumps to the session if it
# comes within this many seconds. 0 = never jump on focus.
# @label=Notification click window @group=status @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=seconds
#FLEET_NOTIFY_JUMP_SECS=60

# Closing the task you are on (reap, exit, kill) lands on its NEIGHBOUR in the
# task sidebar — the next row, or the previous one when it was the last — instead
# of tmux's pick, which is usually the hub (issue #900). A sleeping task is
# skipped, never woken; with no other task left you land on the hub as before.
# F9 / ⌂ / prefix g are never rewritten. 0 = today's behaviour exactly.
# @label=Closing a task lands on the next task @group=status @tier=common @scope=fleet @edit=bool
#FLEET_CLOSE_LANDS_NEXT=1

# ⌂ / F9 land on the task bar first (issue #899): in a task that shows the task
# bar, the first press gives the bar the keyboard (like prefix E) and stays in the
# task; a second press goes on to the hub. In a task with NO task bar on screen
# (narrow client, or prefix e turned it off) the first press opens the task picker
# popup instead (issue #902), where F9 / [⌂ hub] goes on to the hub. A zoomed task
# still goes straight home. 0 = both always go straight to the hub.
# @label=⌂/F9 go to the task bar first @group=status @tier=common @scope=fleet @edit=bool
#FLEET_HOME_SIDEBAR_FIRST=1

# The full-screen list window (issue #1533). 0 (default): there is none — the
# task list on the left is the only list; ⌂ / F9 / prefix g all land on it, focused
# (shown and switched on if needed; F9 a third time hides it), and a fleet's
# resting window is `home`, a plain shell the list draws beside.
# FLEET_HOME_SIDEBAR_FIRST does nothing then. 1 = the old `plan` hub window
# running the dash, and the old ⌂ / F9 / prefix g — kept one batch as the way
# back; the next batch deletes it.
# @label=Full-screen list window (old hub) @group=status @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=bool
#FLEET_DASH_WINDOW=0

# The status bar draws the current window's account only once its quota reaches
# this % on max(5h, week) (issue #1616: the bar draws only what wants your hand —
# below the line the account takes no room). Only the window(s) past the line are
# drawn, coloured by FLEET_ACCOUNT_WARN_PCT / FLEET_ACCOUNT_CEILING. GLOBAL, like
# the container below: the status bar reads the global env only.
# @label=Status-bar quota line (%) @group=status @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int
#FLEET_STATUS_QUOTA_PCT=80

# Optional: a docker container name to show as ○ in the status bar right side
# while it is down (nothing while it runs, issue #1616).
# GLOBAL: the status bar (bin/tmux-status.sh) reads this from the global env only
# — a per-fleet override would be a silent no-op — so it is machine-wide (like the
# FLEET_DISK_* thresholds the same status bar renders).
# @label=Status-bar container @group=status @tier=advanced @scope=global @edit=str
#FLEET_STATUS_CONTAINER="claude-dev"

# How long the status bar's container reading (`docker ps`) is shared between
# attached clients, in seconds (issue #890). tmux renders the bar once per
# status-interval PER CLIENT, so two terminals used to measure twice for the same
# machine-wide answer; now the first renderer measures and publishes
# ${TMPDIR}/fleet-status.cache, and the rest read it (load / memory / disk left
# the bar in issue #1616: fleet-alerts.sh measures them). Default 5 = status-interval.
# 0 = no sharing (every client measures, the pre-#890 behaviour). GLOBAL, like the
# container above.
# @label=Status-bar stats sharing (s) @group=status @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int
#FLEET_STATUS_CACHE_SECS=5

# Subscription-limit severity in the usage + account modal (prefix u; issue
# #239). The official weekly/N-hour limit % (scraped into the ratelimit cache
# while fresh) is graded ok / warn at/above WARN% / crit at/above CRIT%, and the
# modal's limit line takes that color. (It used to color a 5h/7d usage stat on
# the status bar; issue #1100 dropped the stat.) Thresholds (percent of the
# official limit); defaults shown.
# @label=Usage warn % @group=status @tier=advanced @scope=global @edit=int @unit=%
#FLEET_USAGE_WARN_PCT=75
# @label=Usage crit % @group=status @tier=advanced @scope=global @edit=int @unit=%
#FLEET_USAGE_CRIT_PCT=90
# Staleness window for the scraped official limit % (seconds; default 21600 =
# 6h). Past this the modal drops the limit line — a
# stale limit % is worse than none. Shared with the collector's scrape.
# @label=Ratelimit staleness @group=status @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_RATELIMIT_TTL=21600

# Handoff destination (/fleet-handoff, commands/fleet-handoff.md, issue #275).
# Where the handoff doc is STORED so a cleared session can resume from it:
#   comment  (default) — when the pane is issue-bound (a worker's @issue),
#            post the doc as a durable
#            `<!-- fleet:handoff -->`-marked issue COMMENT. It outlives the
#            worktree teardown a committed doc/handoff/*.md does not, and pickup
#            self-resolves it from the pane's @issue (no path to remember). A raw
#            scratch / no-issue pane still falls back to a local file.
#   file     — force the local-file convention for EVERY pane (no comment ever).
#            Use on a SENSITIVE repo where even a scrubbed handoff must not be
#            pushed to a public issue thread.
# Empty is treated as the default (comment).
# @label=Handoff destination (comment|file) @group=handoff @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=enum
#FLEET_HANDOFF_DEST=comment

# Handoff length line (issue #1322). Every line of a handoff doc is context the
# pickup session spends before it works, so /fleet-handoff runs
# `bin/fleet-handoff-file.sh check` on the composed doc before storing it: over
# this many lines — or pasting FLEET_HANDOFF_COPY_LINES (5) lines of the issue
# body the pickup can read for itself — prints a HANDOFF-CHECK hint so the session
# trims and rewrites. Advice only, never a gate. 0 = no line cap.
# @label=Handoff max lines @group=handoff @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=int
#FLEET_HANDOFF_MAX_LINES=200

# Auto-handoff threshold (issue #330). When a worker/scratch session's context
# window crosses this %, its Stop hook (bin/set-claude-state.sh) nudges the model
# to run the /fleet-handoff cycle automatically (store a durable handoff → /clear
# → resume clean) instead of waiting for the operator to notice the statusline bar
# or hitting Claude's lossy near-limit auto-compaction. Reuses the WHOLE existing
# handoff cycle — only the trigger is new. The nudge fires once per session (a
# @handoff_armed latch, reset at the next SessionStart), only from a clean `done`
# (never a needs-attention turn), and only on panes that HAVE /fleet-handoff
# (issue-bound workers + raw scratch; panels and the hub pane are excluded).
# Spends tokens (the handoff doc + the resume). Default 80 (issue #1571: the
# ladder is compact in place from 55%, at most 3 times, hand off at 80%); 0 = OFF
# (mirrors FLEET_RUNAWAY_CPU_PCT). The Stop hook reads it from
# THIS file (and the per-fleet overlay) via bin/fleet-hook-conf.sh — nothing to
# export; `fleet-doctor.sh` shows what the hook sees (issue #561).
# @label=Auto-handoff percent @group=handoff @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=int @unit=%
#FLEET_AUTO_HANDOFF_PCT=80       # context % that triggers an auto-handoff; 0 = OFF

# Hold the auto-handoff while the OPERATOR IS TYPING at that pane (issue #571). A
# handoff cycle Esc+`/clear`s the pane a minute after the nudge; typed into an input
# line holding a half-written draft, that submits the draft with "/clear" glued on
# (a single Esc keeps the draft), and a queued message dies with the old session.
# Claude Code hands a hook no draft signal, so the proxy is tmux: an attached client
# whose CURRENT window is the pane's, with a keypress (#{client_activity}) within
# this many seconds ⇒ the Stop hook skips the nudge for that Stop (no latch — the
# next Stop re-judges; @handoff_deferred_ts is stamped so the hold is visible), and
# bin/fleet-handoff-cycle.sh holds its /clear inside its idle deadline (still typing
# at the deadline ⇒ it aborts without clearing and says so). Ceiling: at
# threshold+10 the nudge fires anyway, or a long conversation would defer itself
# into autocompact. 0 = off (clear under the operator's fingers, the pre-#571 way).
# @label=Handoff typing hold @group=handoff @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_HANDOFF_DEFER_SECS=30     # keypress at the pane's window within N s holds the handoff; 0 = off

# Compact a worker IN PLACE before handing it off (issue #1269). A handoff makes
# the fresh session re-read the code and re-learn the task — often dearer than the
# work left. So when a worker's context sits at/over this % but under
# FLEET_AUTO_HANDOFF_PCT (0 there = no upper line), the Stop hook asks it to write a
# recovery map (branch, PR, next step), then types `/compact <keep the map>` at
# the next idle Stop (held while the operator is typing — FLEET_HANDOFF_DEFER_SECS),
# and after the compaction the charter comes back with a "check the map" line.
# Once per climb: re-armed only after the context drops back below this line,
# ≥ 10 minutes between compactions. Progress shows in the window option
# @compact_stage (prep / compacting / restored). Workers only; 0 = off. Default
# 55 (issue #1571): a lower line (35) pinned sessions at 35% and handed them off
# on the compaction cap alone, never using the upper 60% of the window.
# @label=Compact-in-place percent @group=handoff @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=int @unit=%
#FLEET_COMPACT_PREP_PCT=55       # context % that starts an in-place /compact; 0 = OFF

# Compact a session in place at most this many times, then hand it off (issue
# #1316). Every compaction loses a little detail; by the third or fourth a session
# no longer remembers what it agreed to at the start. Each fleet compaction that
# completes bumps the window option @compact_count; once it reaches this number,
# the next time the context reaches FLEET_COMPACT_PREP_PCT the Stop hook sends the
# auto-handoff block (`/fleet-handoff`, cycle mode, "already compacted N times")
# instead of another recovery map. The fresh session after the handoff starts at
# 0. Workers only (codex untouched); 0 = no cap.
# @label=Compactions before handoff @group=handoff @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=int
#FLEET_COMPACT_MAX=3             # in-place compactions per session before a handoff; 0 = no cap

# Carry on by itself after an in-place compaction (issue #1441). The prep step ends
# the turn and /compact leaves the session idle, so without this nothing starts the
# next turn until someone types. On a FLEET compaction only (Claude Code's own
# carries on by itself) bin/fleet-compact-resume.sh submits /fleet-compact-resume
# once the pane is idle — through the mod's inbox, else keystrokes behind the
# operator-typing hold — and skips when a turn is already running, the pane waits
# on a human, a transfer is pending, or it is a Codex pane. Each outcome is a
# `resumed` row in context-ladder.log; fleet-doctor WARNs on a restore with none.
# 0 = off (the session waits for input, as before).
# @label=Resume after compact @group=handoff @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=bool
#FLEET_COMPACT_RESUME=1          # 1 = submit the next turn after an in-place compaction; 0 = off

# Set the two lines above in TOKENS USED instead of a % (issue #1317), so a model
# with a different window size needs no re-tuning: every window is 1M tokens
# today, so a 70% line meant 700k — long after quality had started to slide. Each
# Stop converts tokens / the pane's @ctx_limit (the window size the status line
# stamps) into that session's %, rounded up, and it WINS over the *_PCT key; a pane
# with no @ctx_limit falls back to the *_PCT key. A line at/over the window can
# never fire. `fleet-doctor.sh`'s `handoff` row prints "<tokens> tok → <pct>% of
# <window>" and WARNs unless compact-prep < handoff < Claude's own auto-compaction.
# 0/unset = use the *_PCT key.
# @label=Auto-handoff tokens @group=handoff @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=int @unit=tokens
#FLEET_AUTO_HANDOFF_TOKENS=0     # tokens used that trigger an auto-handoff; 0 = use the PCT key
# @label=Compact-in-place tokens @group=handoff @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=int @unit=tokens
#FLEET_COMPACT_PREP_TOKENS=0     # tokens used that start an in-place /compact; 0 = use the PCT key

# What the HUB gets at the handoff line (issue #1319). The hub is the operator's
# own seat — no auto /fleet-handoff, no /clear that could eat what is being typed.
# notify: at FLEET_AUTO_HANDOFF_PCT (or _TOKENS) the Stop hook stamps @ctx_warn
# (dash row `⚠ ctx`) and sends ONE FLEET_NOTIFY_CMD message pointing at
# /fleet-handoff (the hub's doc goes to FILE storage); never blocks. Cleared once
# the context reads back under the line. Auto-handoff off ⇒ no line ⇒ no warning.
# @label=Hub context action @group=handoff @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=str
#FLEET_HUB_CTX_ACTION=notify     # notify | off

# Re-state a worker's task charter after a context compaction (issue #1266). The
# SessionStart(source=compact) hook bin/refocus-hook.sh hands a WORKER pane a
# ≤1.5 KB `[fleet charter] #<N>` block (issue, repo, branch, PR, one-issue-one-PR,
# how to land) as additionalContext, so a long auto-compacted session doesn't
# drift out of scope or forget to ship. Zero gh calls; hub/scratch never get it.
# 0 = off. Read from THIS file and the per-fleet overlay at hook time.
# @label=Re-state charter after compact @group=handoff @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=bool
#FLEET_REFOCUS=1                 # 1 = re-inject the worker charter after /compact; 0 = off

# Save a recovery map before Claude Code's OWN auto-compaction (issue #1321). With
# the lines above set right the fleet compacts first, but a window that never took
# the fleet's path meets the built-in one unprepared; this PreCompact(auto) hook
# writes the map itself (git, PR, latest issue comment, last prompts), logs
# `native-precompact` on the context ladder, and the refocus reads it back.
# Workers + scratch; the hub only gets the ledger row. 0 = off.
# @label=Save map before native compact @group=handoff @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=bool
#FLEET_PRECOMPACT=1              # 1 = auto-save a recovery map at PreCompact(auto); 0 = off

# The wait-idle ceiling of the handoff cycle (bin/fleet-handoff-cycle.sh): how long
# it waits for the arming turn to end (@claude_state leaves `working`) before it
# ABORTS WITHOUT CLEARING. A big handoff turn can legitimately run over two minutes.
#
# ⚠️ NOT a free number (issue #677). For the case auto-handoff exists to survive —
# a turn that emitted no Stop hook at all (a model cap #580, a crash), which pins
# @claude_state=working forever — the ONLY thing that can open that gate is the
# stuck-working demotion below, which lands at worst
#
#     FLEET_STUCK_WORKING_SECS + 2 × 10s sweep     (140s at the defaults)
#
# Set this at or under that and auto-handoff can NEVER fire on a capped turn, and
# it fails SILENTLY: the session just fills its context and stops. It must also
# stay under FLEET_HANDOFF_HARD_TIMEOUT (300s) minus FLEET_HANDOFF_VERIFY_TIMEOUT
# (25s), or the cycle's own watchdog kills it after /clear but before the pickup.
# `bin/fleet-handoff-invariant.sh` is the check, fleet-doctor.sh prints its verdict,
# and a selftest reds if either default is edited into a dangerous relation.
# @label=Handoff wait-idle ceiling @group=handoff @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_HANDOFF_IDLE_TIMEOUT=240   # must stay well above FLEET_STUCK_WORKING_SECS + 20

# Multiple subscription accounts, with auto-failover when one hits its usage
# limit (the rolling 5-hour "session" window or the weekly cap). Register each
# account as a token FILE — name = label, contents = its OAuth token from
# `claude setup-token` (chmod 600) — in this directory. When the collector sees
# a session print "You've hit your … limit", it marks that account and rotates,
# so NEW sessions launch on a fresh subscription. Empty/absent dir → OFF (the
# fleet uses your single logged-in account exactly as before). See
# docs/MULTI-ACCOUNT.md.
# @label=Account tokens dir @group=identity @tier=internal @scope=identity @edit=no
#FLEET_ACCOUNTS_DIR="$HOME/.config/claude-fleet/accounts"
# Optional: pin the account order/subset (space-separated labels); default is
# every token file, sorted by name.
# @label=Subscription accounts @group=accounts @tier=advanced @scope=global @edit=str
#FLEET_ACCOUNTS="work personal spare"
# Which KIND of subscription this fleet's new sessions run on (issue #1540, EPIC
# #1529 R2). Two kinds share the accounts dir: `local` — this login's own token
# files — and `pool` — accounts the cross-machine hub leases out (a `hub:<label>`
# file, docs/FLEET-HUB.md). `any` (the default) is the pick as it always was.
# One session chooses for itself with `dash-issue-session.sh --account
# local|pool|any`, which wins over this line; the choice is stamped on the
# window (@account_class) and travels with a remote placement, so the machine
# that opens the session honours it. `pool` with no pool account on the login
# refuses to launch rather than fall back to the login's own subscription.
# Off the default config-panel face (@tier=internal, issue #1101 — the face is at
# its 120-row cap, and the per-session flag is the usual way to say this); the
# panel's `--all` / FLEET_CONFIG_SHOW_INTERNAL=1 edits it.
# @label=Account class @group=accounts @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=enum
#FLEET_ACCOUNT_CLASS=any
# Unified per-session subscription failover. Existing account/quota, transfer and
# quotawatch components remain the owners. Prefer another eligible subscription
# of the current agent, then the allowed other agent; all unavailable => wait.
# Codex logins/readings come from ccquota codex list + budget --source codex.
# Existing FLEET_CODEX_ACCOUNTS labels/homes restrict the local Codex candidates.
# Native resume and manual provider-specific CLI flags keep their agent.
# @label=Subscription failover @group=accounts @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=bool
#FLEET_FAILOVER=0
# Comma-separated allowed agents; quota targets must be fresh, authenticated and
# below FLEET_ACCOUNT_CEILING. No switch to unknown quota or pay-as-you-go credits.
# @label=Failover allowed agents @group=accounts @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=str
#FLEET_FAILOVER_AGENTS=claude,codex
# Background commands (a dev server, a long test) veto a move like they veto
# hibernation. After a HARD wall the session cannot act on them anyway, so once
# its request has waited this long they are recorded, stopped after /exit and
# named in the resume prompt (issue #871). Proactive moves and sleep keep the
# veto. 0 = never override (the old behaviour).
# @label=Hard-wall background grace @group=accounts @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_FAILOVER_BG_GRACE=600
# A request that records the SAME reason this many attempts in a row (one per
# ~60s tick) is stuck: FLEET_NOTIFY_CMD fires once per stuck episode (window,
# account, reason, the unstick command), the dash row shows `⚠ stuck` and
# fleet-doctor counts it. A new reason restarts the count (issue #872).
# @label=Failover stuck after @group=accounts @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=int @unit=attempts
#FLEET_FAILOVER_STUCK_ATTEMPTS=5
# How long a limited account stays out of rotation before it's eligible again
# (seconds; default 18000 = the 5-hour session window).
# @label=Account limit re-check @group=accounts @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_ACCOUNT_LIMIT_TTL=18000
# Pre-emptive rotation via ccquota (issue #513). The banner path above is
# reactive — a session has to be walled first. With a ccquota hub reachable
# (`export` the URL HERE so every fleet script and daemon sees it; ccquota reads
# the viewer token from ~/.ccquota/viewer-token, or CCQUOTA_VIEWER_TOKEN) the
# quota watch (bin/fleet-quotawatch.sh — its OWN 60s daemon
# com.claude-fleet.quotawatch since issue #551, and also run first thing by every
# collector tick) reads each pool account's EXACT 5h/7d utilization and, per account:
#   • prefers the eligible account with the most headroom for NEW sessions
#     (kept while within FLEET_ACCOUNT_PICK_HYST points of the best, so spawns
#     don't flip-flop) — see FLEET_ACCOUNT_PICK below for what "most" means;
#   • at FLEET_ACCOUNT_WARN_PCT messages every session running on it — over its
#     own peer inbox, the SendMessage channel — that a move is coming, so it
#     can commit WIP;
#   • at FLEET_ACCOUNT_CEILING benches it until ccquota's reset instant and
#     moves its sessions (`fleet-account.sh migrate --account`), BEFORE any
#     limit banner. The ceiling is the higher of the 5h and 7d utilizations.
# Fail-open: no ccquota on PATH, no URL, hub unreachable → the banner path,
# unchanged. `fleet-account.sh quota` shows what the watch sees.
# @label=ccquota hub URL @group=accounts @tier=advanced @scope=global @edit=str
#export CCQUOTA_HUB_URL="http://100.x.y.z:8787"
# Your sessions on the OTHER machines, in this sidebar (issue #1423, EPIC #1419):
# with `export CCQUOTA_FLEET=1` too, the collector keeps a 10s refresh of the hub's
# fleet_sessions (bin/fleet-hub-sessions.sh — never on the dash's render path) and
# the dash mixes those rows in with this machine's, nested under their parents,
# drawn exactly like the local ones (issue #1475: no machine tag — the row menu's
# title, `侧边栏 · 在 m4`, names the machine); the top line of the
# sidebar is the machine status line, `● m5 22 · ● m4 3` (online ●, lost ○ with
# how long it has been silent); a machine the hub has lost — or a cache gone
# stale — keeps its rows, dimmed, in their own group at the bottom,
# `─ m4 失联 3 分钟 ─`. Read-only: no key acts on them.
# WHO the hub shows you (issue #1475): your own connection certificate
# (~/.ssh/fleet-cert, from `fleet login`) first — the hub then lists exactly the
# machines your active accounts are on, no token needed; without one (or past
# its validity) the viewer token (CCQUOTA_VIEWER_TOKEN / ~/.ccquota/viewer-token,
# the operator's); neither: nothing is fetched, no remote row, one WARN in
# fleet-doctor. The hub's URL is CCQUOTA_HUB_URL, else FLEET_HUB_URL, else the
# `url` in ~/.config/claude-fleet/hub.json (what `fleet login` wrote).
# The machine label is the hostname's first label unless named here, `host=label`
# pairs.
# @label=Machine labels @group=accounts @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=str
#FLEET_NODE_ALIASES="macmini=m5 mini2=m4"
# Enter on one of those rows steps INTO it (issue #1424): a proxy window here,
# `m4 <name>` (its pane header says the same), attached over ssh to that
# session's window on m4 — typing and
# scrolling are the remote's; closing it only drops the connection; a drop
# reconnects (through the hub relay, `fleet connect --proxy`, when one is set up).
# One proxy window per machine. The ssh host is the machine label itself (as your
# ~/.ssh/config names it) unless mapped here, `label=host` pairs.
# @label=Machine ssh hosts @group=accounts @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=str
#FLEET_REMOTE_SSH="m4=m4 m5=m5"
# Where the sidebar's LIST comes from (issue #1480, EPIC #1479 C1). `local`:
# this machine's windows from tmux, plus your sessions on the other machines from
# the hub's cache above — today's path. `hub`: the WHOLE list is the hub's
# fleet_sessions (that same cache, which since #1480 also holds this machine's
# rows, each with the window it lives in) — so m5 and m4 show one list, in one
# order, with one set of marks; this machine's rows are simply the ones whose
# machine is this one. A local row is still painted off its own tmux window —
# state, needs, glyph, fold, pin, Enter are exactly today's — only WHICH rows is
# the hub's: a window the hub has not seen yet (≤ 15 s after a spawn) is not on
# the list yet, your own sidebar's window excepted. Sidebar only (the hub list,
# prefix+F9, keeps its local rows); needs CCQUOTA_FLEET=1 and a cache on disk,
# else `hub` behaves as `local`. `hub` also switches the STATUS BAR (issue
# #1482): its left part becomes the machine the current window's session is on
# (this one, or the proxy window's), that window's account quota and the hub
# (`m4 ● │ CPU 16% │ MEM 4.0G/16.0G │ ◉ icloud 5h 63% 周 65% │ 入口 ●`), and the
# window list goes blank — back to today's bar, and the list restored, on `local`.
# @label=Sidebar list source @group=status @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=str
#FLEET_SIDEBAR_SOURCE=local
# The list's ORDER (issue #1750). `born` (default): a row sits where its session
# was born — repo group, then 置顶, then its parent's subtree, then birth time
# (@born, stamped at spawn and kept across a move) — so a turn going working →
# done, a `needs` coming and going, or a window closing in the middle moves no
# row, and this machine's rows and the other machines' share one ruler. The rows
# waiting on you stay in place, red; one line above the list counts them (`! 2 个
# 在问你 · ⌃k 跳过去`) and the task list's ⌃k on an empty line walks onto them.
# `status`: the old order — needs on top, then done, working, looping — byte for
# byte, and no summary line.
# @label=List order @group=status @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=str
#FLEET_DASH_ORDER=born
# @label=Rotate early at @group=accounts @tier=advanced @scope=global @edit=int @unit=%
#FLEET_ACCOUNT_CEILING=85
# @label=Warn sessions at @group=accounts @tier=advanced @scope=global @edit=int @unit=%
#FLEET_ACCOUNT_WARN_PCT=70
# Pool accounts no NEW session may land on (issue #2083), space-separated: the
# hub's 「暂停订阅」 (pool.paused.<account>, #1990) arrives on its own — the quota
# tick fetches the hub's client-settings `pool` list into account.paused — so set
# this only to pause one on THIS machine by hand. Only a hub-managed (`hub:`)
# account can be paused; a session already on it runs to its end.
# @label=Paused pool accounts @group=accounts @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=str
#FLEET_ACCOUNT_PAUSED=
# @label=ccquota cache TTL @group=accounts @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_ACCOUNT_QUOTA_TTL=60
# Staleness alarm for the watch (issue #551): every fleet-quotawatch tick restamps
# the ccquota cache (account.quota.ts) — even an unreachable hub restamps — so a
# stamp older than this means NO tick has run for that long and the 70%/85%
# rotation is blind (2026-09-11: 2.5h blind ⇒ a whole 5h window lost). Surfaced
# as `⚠ quota stale 47m` on the status bar, a FAIL in fleet-doctor, and a one-off
# FLEET_NOTIFY_CMD from the next tick that does run. Default 600 = 10× the TTL.
# @label=Quota watch stale after @group=accounts @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_ACCOUNT_QUOTA_STALE=600
# Status-line readings (issue #1267): each window's status line stamps its
# account's 5h/7d % (@rl5h/@rl7d/@rl_reset/@rl_ts), and the watch acts on the
# NEWEST reading per account — a stamp, or ccquota's row as of its fetch. A stamp
# older than this is no reading (an idle session stops re-rendering), and
# ccquota's row stands. Default 600 s.
# @label=Status-line quota reading valid for @group=accounts @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_QUOTA_RL_TTL=600
# The OTHER way that watch goes blind (issue #684): the fetch restamps the cache
# even when it came back with nothing, so a hub answering with zero rows leaves a
# cache that is FRESH and EMPTY — stamp-age alarms read it as healthy while the
# 70%/85% rotation has nothing to act on (2026-09-15: six minutes of it, with
# `--status` saying `fresh 117` and fleet-doctor PASSing). One empty read is noise
# — a hub blip, a fetch killed on its budget — so the STREAK is the verdict: this
# many consecutive empty fetches with a pool + hub configured raises
# `⚠ quota blind 6m` on the status bar, a FAIL on fleet-doctor's qwatch line, and
# one FLEET_NOTIFY_CMD per episode. Default 3 ≈ 3 min at the 60s TTL. 0 = off.
# @label=Quota watch blind after @group=accounts @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=reads
#FLEET_ACCOUNT_QUOTA_BLIND_STREAK=3
# A refused or silent hub does not blind the pick (issue #2465): an empty fetch
# keeps the last good reading (account.quota.read_at says when it was read) and
# the pick ranks on it while it is younger than this; the doctor's qwatch line
# says 「沿用 N 分钟前的读数」 and why (拒 401 · 失联 · 盲). Past it the reading
# is cleared and the blind alarm above takes over. 0 = carry nothing.
# @label=Carry last quota reading @group=accounts @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_QUOTA_STALE_OK=1800
# How long the hub may answer the quota read 401 (while the reading is carried)
# before `✖ quota · refused` and a FAIL on the qwatch line: a refused credential is
# something to fix, not a blip to ride out.
# @label=Quota refused alarm after @group=accounts @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_QUOTA_REFUSED_ALARM=300
# A subscription limit banner is a HINT when ccquota has a fresh reading (issue
# #874): it forces one refetch, and `fleet-account.sh quota-verdict` decides. That
# refetch is skipped while the cache is younger than this, so N walled windows in
# one collector tick (or the failover controller right after it) cost one fetch.
# @label=Banner refetch dedupe @group=accounts @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_ACCOUNT_VERDICT_REFETCH=20
# When ccquota has NO fresh reading (stale / blind / account not on the hub), a
# banner still benches by itself — the path that false-benched healthy accounts
# twice — so the status bar shows `⚠ quota via banner` for this long after the
# last time it happened (issue #874).
# @label=Quota via banner shown for @group=accounts @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_ACCOUNT_QUOTA_VIA_BANNER_SECS=600
# How a new spawn RANKS the eligible accounts once the ceiling has thrown out the
# ones that are too hot to use at all (issues #598, #1231):
#   pace    (default) score = 5h-headroom × 2 + (100 − weekly pace) × 20. The
#           weekly PACE is 7d% − ceiling × (fraction of the 7d window elapsed):
#           + = ahead of an even burn of the week's spendable budget, − = behind.
#           Behind ranks UP — that budget evaporates at the weekly reset the way
#           an idle 5h window does at its own — and a lead of PACE_LEAD points
#           (10) is worth an entire 5h window, so the 5h window still decides
#           between accounts on the same pace (#598's answer) and the week
#           decides beyond that. Why: under `5h` the flat 7d term never outweighed
#           a fresh 5h window, so spawns went to whichever account was CLOSEST to
#           exhausting its week until each hit the ceiling in turn — on
#           2026-09-25 three of four accounts benched for 1–3 days while ~45% of
#           the fourth's week sat unused.
#   5h      the #598 ranking: score = 5h-headroom × 2 + 7d-headroom. The 5-hour
#           window counts double because it EXPIRES — whatever a window does not
#           spend evaporates at its reset and can never be recovered. Kept as the
#           one-line rollback from `pace`.
#   minmax  the pre-#598 ranking, ccquota's headroom_pct = 100 - max(5h, 7d).
#           It reads the two windows as one budget, and on 2026-09-13 that scored
#           `5h 0% · 7d 80%` (20) BELOW `5h 77% · 7d 51%` (23): every spawn piled
#           onto the account that was already burning, while the other account's
#           whole 5-hour window went unspent and then reset. Kept as a one-line
#           rollback, not as a recommendation.
# @label=Spawn ranking @group=accounts @tier=advanced @scope=global @edit=str
#FLEET_ACCOUNT_PICK=pace
# Keep the CURRENT account while it is within this many points of the best, so
# new spawns don't flip-flop between near-equal accounts. Points are 5h-headroom
# equivalents (the score above), so 10 ≈ "10% of a 5-hour window".
# @label=Spawn ranking hysteresis @group=accounts @tier=advanced @scope=global @edit=int
#FLEET_ACCOUNT_PICK_HYST=10
# The weekly PACE rails (issue #1231; `pace` mode only). LEAD: a weekly lead of
# this many points outweighs a whole 5h window in the score. HOLD / MARGIN: an
# account more than HOLD points ahead, or with its 7d within MARGIN of the
# ceiling, is HELD for new spawns — fail-open, like a phase slot: it can never be
# the reason a spawn has no account. REBALANCE: the quota watch moves ONE idle
# session per fleet off the most-ahead account when it leads the current pick by
# this many points (the pick must itself be on pace, with 5h room), at most once
# per COOLDOWN seconds; 0 = no moves (the pace table is still written).
# SPREAD_WARN: the status bar shows `⚠ quota pace spread N` when the most-ahead
# and most-behind accounts are more than this many points apart.
# @label=Weekly lead worth a 5h window @group=accounts @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=points
#FLEET_ACCOUNT_PACE_LEAD=10
# @label=Hold spawns when ahead by @group=accounts @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=points
#FLEET_ACCOUNT_PACE_HOLD=25
# @label=Hold spawns within of ceiling @group=accounts @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=points
#FLEET_ACCOUNT_PACE_MARGIN=5
# @label=Rebalance an idle session at a lead of @group=accounts @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=points
#FLEET_ACCOUNT_PACE_REBALANCE=15
# @label=Rebalance cooldown @group=accounts @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_ACCOUNT_PACE_COOLDOWN=600
# @label=Pace spread warning @group=accounts @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=points
#FLEET_ACCOUNT_PACE_SPREAD_WARN=30
# 5h-WINDOW PHASE STAGGER (issue #598). N subscriptions first used at around the
# same time keep their 5h windows in the same phase: they burn down together and
# reset together, so the pool's total headroom is a sawtooth whose trough is a
# full outage. `fleet-account.sh phase --plan --apply` staggers the accounts that
# have NO live window by 5h/N — a queue of START SLOTS, since a window's phase is
# set by when the account is first used and cannot be set any other way. Benched
# accounts and accounts already mid-window are left out; a slot is always
# fail-open (it can never be the reason a spawn has no account to run on).
#   `FLEET_ACCOUNT_PHASE=0`      ignore any written plan entirely (kill switch)
#   `FLEET_ACCOUNT_PHASE_AUTO=1` let the quota watch re-plan once per window,
#                               instead of only when you run it by hand (OFF by
#                               default: this is the tick that keeps the fleet
#                               alive, so arming it is a deliberate act)
# @label=Honour the phase plan @group=accounts @tier=advanced @scope=global @edit=bool
#FLEET_ACCOUNT_PHASE=1
# @label=Auto re-plan the phase @group=accounts @tier=advanced @scope=global @edit=bool
#FLEET_ACCOUNT_PHASE_AUTO=0

# The FIRST PROMPT a moved session is resumed with (bin/fleet-migrate.sh, issue
# #620 lifting it out of the script). A migrate is a close + `claude --resume`, so
# this text lands at the tail of a transcript that may be forty turns long: it has
# to say WHY the turn stopped, where to re-orient, and — the part that is easy to
# lose — what language to keep speaking, because a model follows its most recent
# instruction and everything the fleet injects is English.
#
#   FLEET_MIGRATE_NUDGE        the subscription-cap move (account A → account B)
#   FLEET_MIGRATE_NUDGE_MODEL  the per-model cap relaunch (#524); `__MODEL__` is
#                              substituted with the model it was relaunched on
#
# A value here REPLACES the built-in entirely — language rule included. If your
# fleet is not English-only, end your text with a sentence of the same shape:
# "Continue replying in the language this session was using before this
# interruption." (bin/fleet-lang.sh holds the canonical wording.) Keep the value
# free of single quotes and backticks: it is embedded single-quoted into the
# launch command line (fleet-migrate.sh strips them anyway, mangling your text).
# Unset ⇒ the built-in: the pre-#620 wording with the language rule appended.
# `--nudge <text>` on one migrate still overrides both, verbatim.
# @label=Migrate resume nudge @group=accounts @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=str
#FLEET_MIGRATE_NUDGE=""          # empty ⇒ the built-in in bin/fleet-migrate.sh
# @label=Migrate resume nudge (model cap) @group=accounts @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=str
#FLEET_MIGRATE_NUDGE_MODEL=""    # empty ⇒ the built-in in bin/fleet-migrate.sh

# Detached-attention escalation: when a session is blocked on your input for
# FLEET_ESCALATE_AFTER seconds and NO tmux client is attached, the collector
# runs FLEET_NOTIFY_CMD with the message as $1. Plug in any notifier:
# A ready-made WeCom 群机器人 notifier ships in extras/ (put the webhook URL in
# ~/.config/claude-fleet-webhook, chmod 600):
# GLOBAL: its readers — the collector (bin/tmux-dash-collect.sh), the disk guard
# (bin/fleet-diskguard.sh), the quota watch (bin/fleet-quotawatch.sh) and the
# install-sync daemon (bin/fleet-install-sync.sh: one message when this login
# stops following stable, issue #1125) — are single machine-wide daemons that
# read this from the global env; a per-fleet override never reaches them (silent
# no-op), so it is machine-wide like its companion FLEET_ESCALATE_AFTER.
# @label=Notification command @group=notify @tier=common @scope=global @edit=path
#FLEET_NOTIFY_CMD="$HOME/.claude/fleet/extras/wecom-webhook-notify.sh"
# @label=Escalate unattended after @group=notify @tier=advanced @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_ESCALATE_AFTER=300

# --- Auto-refuse a blocked command (No only; OFF by default) -----------------
# A `needs` window may be parked on a PERMISSION prompt, and a permission prompt is
# mid-turn for its whole life: a SendMessage queues underneath it, the issue bridge
# only relays a message, and a message cannot press a key (issue #640). So an
# unattended worker can sit there until a human walks over.
#
# bin/fleet-permission.sh --show always works and needs no knob: it makes the
# blocked command and the prompt's own reason readable WITHOUT attaching to the
# pane (dash ⌃k falls through to it on a `⊘` row).
#
# This knob arms the other half — `--deny`, which presses **No, and only ever No**.
# Refusing a blocked operation destroys nothing; it hands control back to the
# worker, which can then rewrite the command safely. Nothing in the fleet may press
# Yes, and no setting turns that on. The digit comes from the row the SCREEN labels
# `No` and is re-asserted as a refusal before a key is sent; a missing, ambiguous or
# Yes-only dialog refuses with nothing sent. Leave unset unless you actually want
# unattended workers unstuck without you.
# @label=Allow auto-refusing a permission prompt @group=notify @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=bool
#FLEET_ALLOW_AUTO_DENY=1        # unset/0 ⇒ --deny prints what it WOULD press, sends nothing

# Disk-pressure guard (bin/fleet-diskguard.sh). The tmux server is shared by
# every fleet, so a full volume ENOSPCs the collector and kills the server —
# taking ALL fleets down at once. The guard measures the volume backing $TMPDIR
# (where the dash cache lives) and:
#   • GATE — fleet-up (spawn) and fleet-restore --if-down (auto-restore) refuse
#     to add load when free < FLEET_DISK_FLOOR_GB, so auto-restore can't rebuild
#     fleets straight back into a full disk (the restore⇄crash loop).
#   • WATCH — the com.claude-fleet.diskguard daemon (60s) captures a forensic
#     incident (open-but-deleted fds, largest recent files, top RSS) under
#     ~/.config/claude-fleet/diskguard/ and fires FLEET_NOTIFY_CMD when free
#     drops below FLEET_DISK_WARN_GB. Defaults shown:
# @label=Stop spawning below disk GB @group=disk @tier=advanced @scope=global @edit=int @unit=GB
#FLEET_DISK_FLOOR_GB=12       # gate refusal threshold

# --- Quota guard (autofill only; OFF by default) -----------------------------
# Autofill spends the subscription its spawned sessions run on. Without this the
# dispatcher keeps filling slots while a weekly window closes, and the operator
# finds out when their OWN session is refused.
#
# Needs ccquota — the binary from TokenLedger
# (https://github.com/verkyyi/claude-fleet/tree/master/tokenledger); see docs/INSTALL.md step 6 for how to
# get it — with a hub configured —
# CCQUOTA_HUB_URL / CCQUOTA_VIEWER_TOKEN in the daemon's environment. With
# ccquota absent or no hub set, every mode is a no-op and the fleet behaves
# exactly as it did before. The gate FAILS OPEN: an unreachable hub or an
# unreadable limit proceeds and says why, like the disk guard.
#
#   bin/fleet-quotaguard.sh --status    # what it would decide right now
# @label=Stop autofill near the quota ceiling @group=quota @tier=advanced @scope=global @edit=bool
#FLEET_QUOTA_GATE=0           # 1 to arm (default 0 = report only, never blocks)
# @label=Quota ceiling @group=quota @tier=advanced @scope=global @edit=int @unit=%
#FLEET_QUOTA_CEILING=90       # hold at/above this utilization %
# @label=Subscription to judge @group=quota @tier=advanced @scope=global @edit=str
#FLEET_QUOTA_ACCOUNT=         # a subscription uuid, or `all`; default: the
#                             # account this machine is logged into
# @label=Path to ccquota @group=quota @tier=advanced @scope=global @edit=path
#FLEET_QUOTA_BIN=             # default: whatever `ccquota` resolves to on PATH
# @label=Disk warning GB @group=disk @tier=advanced @scope=global @edit=int @unit=GB
#FLEET_DISK_WARN_GB=15        # watch capture + notify threshold
# (The volume the guard measures and the min seconds between incident captures are
# fixed internal constants now — the guard MUST measure the $TMPDIR volume the dash
# cache writes to, and the capture cooldown is plumbing no one tunes. A stale
# FLEET_DISK_TARGET / FLEET_DISK_COOLDOWN in an old conf is still honored by
# bin/fleet-diskguard.sh but is no longer a surfaced knob — issue #237.)

# Runaway-CPU watchdog (same daemon). The shared tmux server can also be
# overloaded by a detached orphan spinning a core (issue #151, crash #3: a
# since-fixed hang from an already-removed worktree pegged 100% for ~1h). The
# --watch tick flags OUR-USER processes at/above FLEET_RUNAWAY_CPU_PCT % CPU held
# for >= FLEET_RUNAWAY_CPU_SECS with NO controlling terminal (a reparented orphan;
# live worker panes have a pts and are never touched — the tmux server + launchd/
# systemd are excluded outright). It then captures a forensic incident under
# ~/.config/claude-fleet/diskguard/ and fires FLEET_NOTIFY_CMD; with ACTION=kill it
# also SIGTERM→SIGKILLs the runaway. OFF by default — set PCT>0 to enable.
# @label=Runaway CPU percent @group=disk @tier=advanced @scope=global @edit=int @unit=%
#FLEET_RUNAWAY_CPU_PCT=0        # %CPU that counts as hot; 0 = watchdog OFF
# @label=Runaway CPU seconds @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_RUNAWAY_CPU_SECS=300     # seconds sustained-hot before it's a runaway
# @label=Runaway CPU action @group=disk @tier=advanced @scope=global @edit=str
#FLEET_RUNAWAY_CPU_ACTION=notify  # notify (forensics+notify only) | kill (also SIGTERM/KILL)

# Orphaned-runaway watchdog (same daemon, issue #697) — ON by default, and the
# only fleet defense that can see a process belonging to no worktree and no pane.
# On 2026-09-15 a worker's hand-written load experiment leaked 8 spinning zsh
# processes; they lived 3h20m at ~70% CPU each as PPID=1 orphans and took the
# machine to load 108 until `ps` itself timed out. fleet_reap_worktree_procs, the
# #353 liveness guard and the #550 pane gate all missed them, because each of
# those finds its targets THROUGH a worktree or a pane.
#
# The watchdog above could not cover it either, and that is structural: its
# discriminator is "no controlling terminal", which is equally true of every
# healthy Claude Bash-tool shell — so it ships OFF and must. This one keys on
# `PPID=1` (only a process whose parent DIED reparents to init) plus an argv
# fingerprint of Claude/fleet shapes, which is precise enough to be on by
# default. It is REPORT-ONLY by default: killing a real long task by mistake is
# worse than missing one. Incidents land in ~/.config/claude-fleet/diskguard/
# and fire FLEET_NOTIFY_CMD; bin/fleet-doctor.sh shows the live state on its
# `machine` line, and `bin/fleet-diskguard.sh --orphans` lists them on demand.
# @label=Orphan runaway CPU percent @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=%
#FLEET_ORPHAN_CPU_PCT=50        # %CPU that counts as hot; 0 = watchdog OFF
# @label=Orphan runaway seconds @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_ORPHAN_CPU_SECS=300      # seconds sustained-hot before it's flagged
# @label=Orphan runaway action @group=disk @tier=advanced @scope=global @edit=str
#FLEET_ORPHAN_CPU_ACTION=notify # notify (report only — the default) | kill (SIGTERM/KILL the process GROUP)
# @label=Extra orphan fingerprint @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=str
#FLEET_ORPHAN_EXTRA_RE=         # an ERE OR'd into the built-in fleet fingerprint

# Orphaned-LISTENER sweep (issue #1154), on the same diskguard --watch tick. An
# agent's `python3 -m http.server` / dev server binds `*` by default and outlives
# its window as a PPID=1 orphan — one was found serving the whole Claude
# scratchpad root to the LAN for two days. The sweep takes a TCP listener whose
# cwd is fleet/Claude-anchored (a scratchpad, a `*-issue-N`/`*-scratch-N`
# worktree even once removed, ~/.claude), whose tree has no tmux/claude ancestor,
# where no live session still works, once it is older than this. It KILLS by
# default (a narrow match, and what it finds is exposure, not waste); `notify`
# only reports. Log: ~/.config/claude-fleet/diskguard/listen-reaped.log.
# `fleet-doctor`'s `listen` line WARNs on any fleet listener on the LAN.
# @label=Orphan listener age @group=disk @tier=advanced @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_ORPHAN_LISTEN_SECS=21600    # 6h; 0 = sweep OFF
# @label=Orphan listener action @group=disk @tier=advanced @scope=global @edit=str
#FLEET_ORPHAN_LISTEN_ACTION=kill   # kill (the default) | notify
# @label=Orphan listener sweep interval @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_ORPHAN_LISTEN_EVERY=300     # min seconds between sweeps
# @label=Listener exemptions @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=str
#FLEET_LISTEN_EXEMPT_RE=           # an ERE of argv never counted/reaped (doc-preview, cloudflared, webhook built in)

# Showing a file on the operator's terminal (issue #1367). bin/fleet-show.sh sends
# an image/PDF/QR code over the existing SSH connection to the iTerm2 the operator
# is attached from (OSC 1337 file transfer → their ~/Downloads), instead of an
# agent `open`-ing it on this machine's screen. It picks the session's client whose
# #{client_termtype} matches FLEET_SHOW_TERM_RE; none → it prints the path.
# @label=Show files on the operator's iTerm2 @group=notify @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=bool
#FLEET_SHOW=1                      # 0 = never send; always print the path instead
# @label=Show-file size cap @group=notify @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=int @unit=bytes
#FLEET_SHOW_MAX_BYTES=20971520     # per file; bigger → the path is printed instead
# @label=Show-file terminal match @group=notify @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=regex
#FLEET_SHOW_TERM_RE=^iTerm2        # ERE on #{client_termtype}
# @label=Show-file inline hold @group=notify @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_SHOW_HOLD_SECS=120          # --inline: how long the drawn screen waits for a key
# @label=Show-file part size @group=notify @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=int @unit=bytes
#FLEET_SHOW_PART_BYTES=768         # base64 bytes per OSC 1337 FilePart

# Opening a link on the operator's computer (issue #1379). bin/fleet-open.sh writes
# an iTerm2 Custom= escape (signed with ~/.config/claude-fleet/open.secret) to the
# same client fleet-show picks; the operator's side (#1380) opens it — a page on
# this machine through an ssh port forward. No iTerm2 → bin/open-url.sh.
# @label=Open links on the operator's iTerm2 @group=notify @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=bool
#FLEET_OPEN=1                      # 0 = never write the escape; straight to open-url.sh
# @label=This machine's ssh alias (fleet-open hint) @group=notify @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=str
#FLEET_OPEN_SSH_HOST=              # the operator's ssh alias for this machine; empty = their side guesses

# Transcript archive (issue #1299), once a day on the same diskguard --watch tick.
# ~/.claude/projects only ever grows (one .jsonl per Claude session); a session
# idle this many days — and a fleet helper `claude -p` transcript (the status
# classifier's) idle a day — is tarred into ~/.config/claude-fleet/transcript-archive/
# and removed. A transcript a restore map, a /fleet-history ledger or a live
# session points at is NEVER moved. Put one back with
# `bin/fleet-transcript-archive.sh --restore <id>`; /fleet-history resume does it
# on its own. Log: ~/.config/claude-fleet/diskguard/transcript-archive.log.
# @label=Transcript keep days @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=day
#FLEET_TRANSCRIPT_KEEP_DAYS=30     # idle days before a session's transcript is archived
# @label=Helper transcript keep hours @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=hour
#FLEET_TRANSCRIPT_HELPER_KEEP_HOURS=24
# @label=Transcript archive @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=bool
#FLEET_TRANSCRIPT_ARCHIVE=1        # 0 = the daily pass is OFF
# @label=Transcript archive interval @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_TRANSCRIPT_ARCHIVE_EVERY=86400  # min seconds between two finished passes
# @label=Transcript archive tick budget @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_TRANSCRIPT_ARCHIVE_BUDGET=30    # seconds of work per tick; a backlog resumes next tick

# Homebrew keg permissions (issue #2283) — the diskguard tick's brew pass. brew
# pours kegs with the caller's umask, so an owner on 077 leaves kegs no other
# login can read (python ssl, tmux). On a machine with 2+ logins, as the brew
# prefix's owner, the pass puts Cellar / opt / keg paths back to go+rX and logs
# each repair in ~/.config/claude-fleet/diskguard/brew-perms.log; the doctor's
# `brew` row WARNs when this login cannot repair them.
# @label=Homebrew keg repair @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=bool
#FLEET_BREW_PERMS=1                # 0 = the pass is OFF
# @label=Homebrew keg repair interval @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_BREW_PERMS_EVERY=300        # min seconds between two passes

# Memory guard (issue #1292) — com.claude-fleet.memguard, a KeepAlive daemon that
# samples every 2s. 2026-10-03 one `git` went from nothing to ~40 GB in two
# seconds and rebooted the machine with every session on it. It only ever touches
# OUR processes (a command an agent / fleet pane started, or a PPID=1 orphan whose
# cwd is a fleet anchor), and never a claude/codex session (recorded only).
#   • SPIKE — RSS grew ≥ SPIKE_GROW_MB within SPIKE_WINDOW seconds AND the machine
#     is at memory-pressure warn/critical → SPIKE_ACTION (SIGKILL that one pid).
#   • HARD — a fleet process at ≥ PROC_HARD_PCT % of physical RAM → SPIKE_ACTION,
#     whatever its growth or the pressure.
#   • ORPHAN — PPID=1 + fleet cwd + > ORPHAN_MB + older than ORPHAN_SECS → ORPHAN_ACTION.
# `bin/fleet-memguard.sh --once --dry-run` shows what it would do right now.
# Evidence: ~/.config/claude-fleet/diskguard/incident-mem-*.log. All @tier=internal:
# the defaults are the design, and the modal's default view is at its row cap.
# @label=Memory guard on @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=bool
#FLEET_MEMGUARD=1                  # 0 = the daemon idles
# @label=Memory spike growth MB @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=MB
#FLEET_MEM_SPIKE_GROW_MB=4096      # growth inside the window that counts as a spike
# @label=Memory spike window @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_MEM_SPIKE_WINDOW=10         # seconds the growth is measured over
# @label=Memory spike action @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=str
#FLEET_MEM_SPIKE_ACTION=kill       # kill (the default) | report — for SPIKE and HARD
# @label=Memory hard ceiling % @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=%
#FLEET_MEM_PROC_HARD_PCT=50        # one fleet process at this % of RAM is killed; 0 = off
# @label=Memory guard exemptions @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=str
#FLEET_MEM_EXEMPT_RE=              # an ERE of argv memguard never kills (recorded as exempt)
# @label=Memory orphan MB @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=MB
#FLEET_MEM_ORPHAN_MB=2048          # an orphan holding more than this…
# @label=Memory orphan age @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_MEM_ORPHAN_SECS=21600       # …for longer than this (6h) is flagged
# @label=Memory orphan action @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=str
#FLEET_MEM_ORPHAN_ACTION=report    # report (the default) | kill
# One claude/codex SESSION that has grown past this many MB of RSS gets a dash
# badge (`⚠ mem 4.3G`, window option @claude_mem_warn) and one notification
# naming it, with the line that moves it to a fresh session via /fleet-handoff.
# Never restarted automatically (issue #1297). Cleared under 90% of the line.
# @label=Fat session warning MB @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=MB
#FLEET_CLAUDE_RSS_WARN_MB=4096     # 0 = off

# Machine admission (issue #1090, EPIC #1291 C4): every path that opens a NEW
# session (dash, backlog Enter, dispatch/autofill, bridge, /fleet-epic-run, the
# scratch pool) asks fleet_session_cap_ok, which now also asks whether the
# MACHINE has room — not just whether the session count does. Memory pressure at
# or above FLEET_ADMIT_PRESSURE, avail% under FLEET_ADMIT_MEM_FREE_PCT, or 1-min
# load/core over FLEET_ADMIT_LOAD_PER_CORE → the spawn is refused as "at
# capacity" (exit 2), the caller retries next tick, and spawns resume on their own
# when the reading drops. Running sessions are untouched; crash restore, quota
# migration and a handoff's continuation never pass this gate. The backlog header
# shows 「暂停开新：内存紧张」 / 「暂停开新：负载过高」 while it holds.
# @label=Machine admission gate @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=bool
#FLEET_ADMIT=1                      # 0 = admit on session counts alone (pre-#1090)
# @label=Admission min free memory % @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=%
#FLEET_ADMIT_MEM_FREE_PCT=15        # hold new sessions below this % available; 0 = off
# @label=Admission memory pressure @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=str
#FLEET_ADMIT_PRESSURE=warn          # warn (default) | critical | off — kernel pressure level that holds
# @label=Admission load per core @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=str
#FLEET_ADMIT_LOAD_PER_CORE=1.5      # hold above this 1-min load/core; 0 = off (default: FLEET_LOADGEN_LOAD_PER_CORE if set, else 1.5)
# Since issue #1831 admission is the ONLY gate by default, so it answers "room for
# one more?" in MB: one session's cost (the median RSS of the live agents × a
# growth factor, never under a floor) must fit above the kept-back floor
# (max(FLEET_ADMIT_MEM_FREE_PCT of RAM, FLEET_ADMIT_RESERVE_MB)) after paying for
# every session admitted in the last FLEET_ADMIT_SETTLE_SECS (or still building
# its window) — so a burst of spawns cannot all read the same free memory. After a
# hold, FLEET_ADMIT_HYST_MB more must free up before it reopens. `fleet-doctor`'s
# `admit` row and the backlog's 「余≈N」 show the room left.
# @label=Admission reserve MB @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=MB
#FLEET_ADMIT_RESERVE_MB=2048        # never hand out the last this-many MB (the % floor above wins when larger)
# @label=Admission recovery MB @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=MB
#FLEET_ADMIT_HYST_MB=1024           # after a hold, this much more must free up before it reopens
# @label=Session cost MB (pinned) @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=MB
#FLEET_ADMIT_SESSION_MB=0           # pin one session's cost; 0 = measure it
# @label=Session cost floor MB @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=MB
#FLEET_ADMIT_SESSION_MB_MIN=512     # a measured cost is never under this
# @label=Session cost growth @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=str
#FLEET_ADMIT_SESSION_GROWTH=3       # median live-agent RSS × this = one session's cost (a transcript grows)
# @label=Admission settle secs @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=s
#FLEET_ADMIT_SETTLE_SECS=120        # an admitted session is reserved this long, until memory shows it

# The load line on `fleet-doctor`'s `machine` check (issue #697): 1-minute load
# average divided by core count. Warns at or above this, so the number means the
# same thing on a 4-core and a 16-core box.
# @label=Doctor load warn per core @group=disk @tier=advanced @scope=global @edit=int
#FLEET_LOAD_WARN_PER_CORE=4

# macOS fseventsd RSS (MB) at which `fleet-doctor`'s `machine` check WARNs and the
# diskguard --watch tick sends its once-a-day reminder (issue #889): a bloated
# fseventsd stalls every new session 6-8s at spawn. Report-only — the fleet never
# kills it; the fix is `sudo killall fseventsd` (launchd restarts it). Linux: unused.
# @label=fseventsd warn MB @group=disk @tier=advanced @scope=global @edit=int @unit=MB
#FLEET_FSEVENTSD_WARN_MB=1024

# `fleet-doctor`'s `memory` / `files` / `pty` lines and the diskguard --watch
# notices (issue #1293, EPIC #1291): memory pressure (level, available %,
# compressor %, swap, the fleet's three largest processes), the open-file table
# (kern.num_files / kern.maxfiles; Linux fs/file-nr) and the pty table (/dev/ttys*
# / kern.tty.ptmx_max; Linux /dev/pts). The doctor WARNs at pressure warn/critical
# or a table at/over its %; the tick sends ONE FLEET_NOTIFY_CMD per edge (normal →
# warn/critical, or a table crossing its line), at most one per kind per cooldown.
# @label=Files table warn % @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=%
#FLEET_FILES_WARN_PCT=80
# @label=Pty table warn % @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=%
#FLEET_PTY_WARN_PCT=80
# @label=Memory notify cooldown @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_MEM_NOTIFY_COOLDOWN=1800

# --- credential proxy (issue #1970, EPIC #1967 C3) ---------------------------
# com.claude-fleet.cred-proxy: this login's one credential proxy. A session holds
# a session credential and talks to 127.0.0.1; the proxy picks direct / relay /
# central from the hub's trust record × the last fleet-node-probe.sh result and
# puts the subscription credential in on the way out (central: none at all).
# Off = today's wiring byte for byte, and no proxy process runs.
# The relay pass is minted per login by fleet-relay-cred.sh (issue #1974,
# docs/CRED-RELAY.md) and kept 0600 in $FLEET_CONF_DIR/cred-proxy/; a hand-set
# FLEET_CRED_RELAY_TOKEN overrides it and lives in secrets.env, never here.
# @label=Credential proxy @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=bool
# A login opened by bin/fleet-login-new.sh (the hub's account op) gets 1 in its own
# fleet.conf [common] from the start, with FLEET_CRED_SEPARATE=1 (issue #2294).
#FLEET_CRED_PROXY=0                # 1 = run the proxy (bin/fleet-cred-proxy.sh); flip it with `fleet cred-proxy enable|disable` (#2134)
# @label=Credential proxy idle check @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_CRED_PROXY_IDLE_SECS=60     # how often the launcher re-reads the switch
# @label=Singapore relay URL @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=str
#FLEET_CRED_RELAY_URL=             # the relay route's base (…/anthropic, …/chatgpt/codex); empty = no relay route
# @label=Central credential proxy URL @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=str
#FLEET_CRED_CENTRAL_URL=           # the central route's base (…/v1/proxy/{anthropic,codex}); empty = the hub
# Per-person budgets (issue #1977): the proxy reports each answer's token usage
# to the hub (POST /v1/node/usage) and refuses this login's person's requests
# while the hub says they are over fleet.person_budget.<principal>
# (`fleet config budget`). 0 = report nothing, refuse nothing.
# @label=Per-person budget reports @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=bool
#FLEET_CRED_BUDGET=1
# @label=Per-person budget report interval @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_CRED_BUDGET_SECS=15         # also how soon an over-budget verdict takes effect
# Separated credentials (issue #1971, EPIC #1967 C4): on a trusted machine, a role
# account (_fleetcred) owns /var/db/fleet-cred/<login>/ — the leased credentials,
# Codex auth.json and node.env — and runs the proxy; a session reading them gets
# "Permission denied". Needs root ONCE (the install pass uses password-less sudo,
# else prints the command); turning it back to 0 moves every file back.
# Needs FLEET_CRED_PROXY=1 (sessions reach the subscription only through it).
# @label=Separated credentials @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=bool
# A NEW login (bin/fleet-login-new.sh) is opened with 1 — separated by root at the
# open, before its first session; the team pool goes into its store (#2294).
#FLEET_CRED_SEPARATE=0             # 1 = bin/fleet-credsep.sh install (docs/CRED-SEPARATE.md)

# `fleet-doctor`'s `spotlight` line (host section, macOS only, issue #1075): WARNs
# while Spotlight still indexes the data volume, naming `sudo mdutil -a -i off`
# (docs/HOST.md#spotlight). Report-only — the doctor never changes system state.
# Set to 0 on a host where you keep Spotlight on deliberately. Linux: unused.
# @label=Doctor Spotlight check @group=disk @tier=advanced @scope=global @edit=bool
#FLEET_DOCTOR_SPOTLIGHT=1

# `fleet-doctor`'s `sleep` line (host section, macOS only, issue #1076): WARNs
# while `pmset -g` shows a non-zero idle-sleep, naming `sudo pmset -a sleep 0
# autorestart 1 womp 1` (docs/HOST.md#headless). Report-only. Set to 0 on a host
# that is meant to sleep (a laptop you carry). Linux: unused.
# @label=Doctor sleep check @group=disk @tier=advanced @scope=global @edit=bool
#FLEET_DOCTOR_SLEEP=1

# `fleet-doctor`'s `siri` line (host section, macOS only, issue #1076): INFO while
# Siri is on for the console user (its helpers stay resident, ~400 MB), pointing
# at the Settings toggle (docs/HOST.md#headless). Report-only. Set to 0 on a host
# where you use Siri. Linux: unused.
# @label=Doctor Siri check @group=disk @tier=advanced @scope=global @edit=bool
#FLEET_DOCTOR_SIRI=1

# `fleet-doctor`'s `icloud` line (host section, macOS only, issue #1076): INFO
# while an iCloud sync daemon (bird / cloudd / fileproviderd) is resident, pointing
# at the sign-out / iCloud Drive toggle (docs/HOST.md#headless). Report-only. Set
# to 0 on a host that keeps its iCloud account deliberately. Linux: unused.
# @label=Doctor iCloud check @group=disk @tier=advanced @scope=global @edit=bool
#FLEET_DOCTOR_ICLOUD=1

# `fleet-doctor`'s `network` line (host section, issue #1081 — macOS AND Linux):
# WARN when one gateway is the default route through two or more interfaces
# (wired + Wi-Fi on one subnet: replies leave by the wrong link, "connected, but
# nothing answers"), naming the Wi-Fi switch-off (docs/HOST.md#network).
# Report-only. Set to 0 on a host that is multi-homed on purpose.
# @label=Doctor network check @group=disk @tier=advanced @scope=global @edit=bool
#FLEET_DOCTOR_NETWORK=1

# `fleet-doctor`'s `ingress` line (issue #1196 — macOS AND Linux): with
# FLEET_SSH_PUBLIC_HOST + FLEET_SSH_PROBE_HOST set, asks the probe host to
# `ssh-keyscan` the public entry and WARNs when it is closed, answers with
# another machine's host key, or the probe itself cannot be reached
# (docs/HOST.md#ingress). Report-only; nothing configured ⇒ no line — which is
# the everyday switch, so this one is internal: 0 keeps the keys (the welcome
# letter reads them) but drops the probe.
# @label=Doctor public-entry check @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=bool
#FLEET_DOCTOR_INGRESS=1
# How long the `ingress` verdict is cached in global/ingress.probe: a PASS for this
# long, a WARN for at most 5 min. 0 = probe on every run.
# @label=Ingress verdict cache @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_INGRESS_TTL=3600
# The `ingress` probe's per-step bound — ssh ConnectTimeout to the probe host and
# ssh-keyscan's -T at the entry; the whole probe is capped at twice this + 10s.
# @label=Ingress probe timeout @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_INGRESS_TIMEOUT=20

# `fleet-doctor`'s `mcp` lines (issue #891): per fleet, its MCP allowlist
# (FLEET_MCP_CONFIG) and what its live claude sessions carry in MCP processes +
# RSS — MCP servers are one copy per session, so the cost multiplies. WARNs for a
# fleet with no allowlist (its sessions inherit every server in ~/.claude.json).
# Report-only. Set to 0 to drop the lines. Cross-platform.
# @label=Doctor MCP check @group=disk @tier=advanced @scope=global @edit=bool
#FLEET_DOCTOR_MCP=1

# Load experiments (bin/fleet-loadgen.sh) — the sanctioned way to put a box under
# CPU pressure, because the hand-written `trap … EXIT` form is what leaked the
# orphans above. Each burner carries its OWN deadline (a kernel alarm(2) armed
# before the exec, plus a $SECONDS bound), so SIGKILLing the parent — or closing
# the pane — cannot leak it. These caps bound a typo:
# @label=Load-gen max burners @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int
#FLEET_LOADGEN_MAX_PROCS=64
# @label=Load-gen max seconds @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_LOADGEN_MAX_SECS=900

# Host gates on top of those caps (issue #922): a charter's `8 900`, run beside
# three fleets, took load to 152 and stalled every fleet daemon for 14-20 min.
# Loadgen REFUSES (exit 3) while the 1-min load per core is already above
# LOAD_PER_CORE (0 = off), and CLAMPS the burner count to CORE_PCT% of the cores
# (min 1; 0 = no clamp). `fleet-loadgen.sh --force` passes both for a deliberate
# saturation run; it never lifts the two caps above.
# @label=Load-gen refuse above load per core @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=str
#FLEET_LOADGEN_LOAD_PER_CORE=1
# @label=Load-gen max share of cores @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=%
#FLEET_LOADGEN_CORE_PCT=50

# Heavy-job queue (bin/fleet-heavy.sh, issue #1295): tests, pre-PR gates and
# pushes run at most SLOTS at once ACROSS EVERY LOGIN on this machine — the rest
# queue. Slots are flocks on /Users/Shared/claude-fleet/heavy/slot-K (Linux:
# /var/tmp/claude-fleet/heavy), released the instant a holder dies. The fleet's
# Bash hook prefixes the wrapper onto any statement whose command matches RE
# (a Python regex; empty = the built-in default: git push, pytest, npm test,
# run-selftests.sh, local-prod-gate.sh, pre-pr.sh) — business repos change
# nothing. A queued job runs anyway after WAIT seconds, with a WARN; a foreground
# Bash call queues at most half its tool timeout. `fleet-heavy.sh --status` lists
# holders and waiters. Set FLEET_HEAVY=0 to stop rewriting.
# @label=Heavy-job queue @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=bool
#FLEET_HEAVY=1
# @label=Heavy-job slots (machine-wide) @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int
#FLEET_HEAVY_SLOTS=3
# @label=Heavy-job max queue wait @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_HEAVY_WAIT=1800
# @label=Heavy-job command regex @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=str
#FLEET_HEAVY_RE=
# LIGHT runs skip the queue (issue #1313), matched before RE: a pytest aimed at a
# file / `::` node / `-k` filter (no xdist -n/--dist), `npm test -- <file|-t>`,
# `run-selftests.sh <name>` (no glob, no option). Empty = that built-in list;
# `(?!)` = nothing is light. `fleet-heavy.sh --status` shows the last 24h's waits.
# @label=Heavy-job light-run regex @group=disk @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=str
#FLEET_HEAVY_LIGHT_RE=

# --- Spawn ergonomics -------------------------------------------------------
# By default a newly spawned worker window is created in the background (tmux
# new-window -d) and NOT selected — you stay on your current window, and the new
# session surfaces via the dash + urgency sorter. Set to 1 to restore the old
# behavior: interactive spawns (backlog Enter / new-session box) jump to the new
# window. Headless spawns never steal focus regardless.
# @label=Jump to new worker windows @group=spawn @tier=common @scope=fleet @edit=bool
#FLEET_SPAWN_FOCUS=0

# --- Worktree location (bin/fleet-lib.sh fleet_worktree_dir, issue #886) ------
# Where NEW worktrees are created — issue spawns, scratch sessions (⌃s and the warm
# pool) and a manual `cw` all ask the same function. Empty (default) = a sibling of
# the base checkout (`<main>-issue-N` next to `<main>`), the historic layout.
#
# Why set it: worktrees are short-lived, but Spotlight indexes each one (checkout +
# dependencies) like any long-lived folder — on the machine that prompted this, 92
# worktrees / 77 GB re-indexed as they churned. Spotlight skips any directory whose
# name ends in `.noindex`, so point this at one, e.g. ~/projects/.fleet-worktrees.noindex
# (fleet-doctor says INFO when the name lacks the suffix). Basenames keep their
# shape (`<main-basename>-issue-N`), so the dash, the seat check and the resume
# ledger recognise them unchanged; retired worktrees go to `<root>/.fleet-trash`.
# Existing worktrees are NOT moved — only new ones land here. A leading ~/ is expanded.
# @label=Worktree root directory @group=spawn @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=path
#FLEET_WORKTREE_ROOT=""

# --- Slow trash purge (bin/fleet-lib.sh fleet_trash_sweep, issue #893) --------
# A retired worktree is renamed into .fleet-trash and deleted later by the cleanup
# daemon's sweep — by default everything in the trash, in one go. One worktree with
# its dependencies is ~200k file deletions, and several at once flood the file-event
# daemon (fseventsd) the same way an install does. Set N to delete at most N trash
# entries per tick, at `nice -n 19`, and to skip the tick while the 1-minute load per
# core is over FLEET_TRASH_PURGE_MAX_LOAD (default 1; 0 = never skip). A closed disk
# gate overrides both — the trash is what frees a full disk. Unset/0 = purge all.
# @label=Trash entries purged per tick @group=janitor @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=int @unit=per tick
#FLEET_TRASH_PURGE_PER_TICK=""
# @label=Trash purge load ceiling (per core) @group=janitor @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=str
#FLEET_TRASH_PURGE_MAX_LOAD=""

# --- Worktree setup hook (bin/fleet-lib.sh fleet_worktree_setup, issue #885) --
# A command every NEW worktree runs right after `git worktree add` (issue spawns and
# scratch sessions), as `<cmd> <worktree> <main>` with cwd = the worktree. Empty
# (default) = nothing runs, the historic behaviour. A relative path resolves against
# the install root, a bare name against its bin/, a leading ~/ against $HOME.
# It can never fail a spawn: a non-zero exit or a timeout is one line in
# logs/worktree-setup.log and the spawn carries on.
#
# The stock hook is bin/fleet-deps-link.sh. Why: each worktree installed its own
# dependencies from zero — on a JS monorepo 3–6 GB and >200,000 files per spawn, all
# of them fseventsd events at install and again at reap, and nearly all byte-identical
# to the base checkout's. With it, a directory whose lockfile matches the base's gets
# `node_modules → <main>/<dir>/node_modules` instead; a differing lockfile, a missing
# base install, or a base node_modules that links back into repo source (workspace /
# file: / link: deps) is left to install as usual. `npm|pnpm|yarn|bun install/add/…`
# in a linked dir is refused by hooks/bash-guard.py — `fleet-deps-link.sh --unlink
# <dir>` first. Cost: tools that cache INSIDE node_modules (.vite, .cache) share that
# cache with the base; a toolchain that resolves real paths sees files under <main>.
# @label=Worktree setup command @group=spawn @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=path
#FLEET_WORKTREE_SETUP=""

# Wall-clock budget for FLEET_WORKTREE_SETUP (fleet_timebox). The spawn waits this
# long at most; a hook still running is killed and logged, the spawn continues.
# @label=Worktree setup timeout @group=spawn @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_WORKTREE_SETUP_TIMEOUT=120

# Keep the BASE checkout's dependencies current with master (issue #961). The link
# above only proves the LOCKFILES match — and base-sync fast-forwards the base every
# 60 s, so they always do, even when the base's node_modules was installed from an
# older lockfile. So fleet-deps-link links only a base tree stamped with the sha of
# the lockfile it was installed from (node_modules/.fleet-lock-sha), and with this
# on, base-sync reinstalls (nice'd, serial, timeboxed, logs/base-deps.log) every
# base dir whose lockfile moved: npm ci / yarn --frozen-lockfile (--immutable on
# yarn 2+) / pnpm --frozen-lockfile. A failed install is logged and retried next
# tick; it never affects the fast-forward. Empty = on exactly when
# FLEET_WORKTREE_SETUP is fleet-deps-link; 1 / 0 force it. Turning shared deps on
# for a fleet: run `bin/fleet-deps-link.sh --prime-base <main>` once to install +
# stamp every base dir; `fleet-doctor` counts fresh / stale / unstamped ones.
# @label=Keep base deps current @group=spawn @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=bool
#FLEET_BASE_DEPS=""

# Wall-clock budget for ONE base-dir reinstall (fleet_timebox); a timed-out install
# is killed, logged, left unstamped (so nothing links to it) and retried next tick.
# @label=Base deps install timeout @group=spawn @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=int @unit=sec
#FLEET_BASE_DEPS_TIMEOUT=900

# --- Warm scratch pool (bin/scratch-pool.sh) ---------------------------------
# How many pre-started scratch sessions to keep parked and ready. 0 (default) = OFF.
#
# Why: pressing ⌃s cold costs ~7s before you can type — `git fetch` + `git worktree
# add` (~3.2s), claude's boot (~1.7s), and then a ~1.1s window in which Claude Code's
# TUI has ALREADY painted its `❯` box but silently DISCARDS everything you type
# (its input handler flushes whatever is in flight when it mounts). The box looking
# ready is the trap. With a warm entry, ⌃s is a `move-window` + rename of a session
# that finished all of that minutes ago: measured 0.46s to the window and 0.30s for
# the first keystroke — 2.9s end to end including the dash's own 3s refresh tick.
#
# Cost: each warm entry is a real idle claude (~550MB RSS) plus its worktree. They
# are parked in a `<fleet>-pool` holding session, which has no plan/dash window, so
# they are invisible to the dash and to every session count. Warming refuses to
# start one when the fleet is already at its session cap.
#
# In a fleet hosting 2+ repos this is PER REPO (issue #797): each hosted repo keeps
# its own warm entries (stamped @repo, all in the one holding session), and a
# scratch only ever claims one of its own repo's. A repo overlay
# (fleets/<sess>/repos/<slug>.conf) may set its own FLEET_SCRATCH_POOL; unset
# there, it takes this fleet value — so N here means N per hosted repo.
#
# ON by default (issue #2233): 1 per hosted repo plus 1 for HOME (a no-repo
# session warmed in $HOME), refilled on the diskguard tick within seconds of a
# claim. A slot only grows while the machine is quiet — load per core at most
# FLEET_POOL_LOAD_PER_CORE (1) and the disk above FLEET_DISK_WARN_GB — and an entry
# warmed before an upgrade is retired, never handed out. 0 turns it off.
# @label=Warm scratch sessions @group=spawn @tier=common @scope=fleet @edit=int
#FLEET_SCRATCH_POOL=1

# Seconds before an unclaimed warm entry is retired and rebuilt. Its worktree is a
# snapshot of origin/<base> taken when it was created, so handing out an old one
# silently branches your work off a stale master.
# @label=Warm entry max age (s) @group=spawn @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=int
#FLEET_POOL_MAX_AGE=1800

# How long ⌃s waits before rebuilding the entry it just consumed. Warming is a full
# cold claude boot; firing it at the instant you start typing into the window you
# just claimed is the one moment that contention is felt.
# @label=Warm pool refill delay (s) @group=spawn @tier=internal @scope=fleet @edit=int
#FLEET_POOL_REFILL_DELAY=45

# --- Dash prompt-line paste guard (bin/dash-enter.sh, issue #531) -----------------
# The dash's always-visible prompt line ("type a task, ↵ → scratch session") is an
# fzf input, and a terminal delivers a MULTI-LINE PASTE as one Enter PER LINE (fzf
# has no bracketed-paste awareness on the input line). Pasting a ~250-line stack
# trace once fired 250 seeded spawns — 244 concurrent `git worktree add`s, the
# global session cap bypassed, disk 30→6 GB. So a typed Enter is DEBOUNCED: an
# Enter that trails the previous one by less than this many ms is treated as a
# paste line and dropped; an isolated Enter defers this long, then spawns. Raising
# it drops more of a fast-typed second task; lowering it lets a slow paste through.
# @label=Prompt-line debounce (ms) @group=spawn @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=ms
#FLEET_SPAWN_GUARD_MS=1000

# How long an in-progress scratch spawn (issue #531) counts against the session cap
# before its window exists — so a flood of spawns can't all pass the cap check
# before any of them lands a window. A crashed spawn's marker ages out after this
# many seconds rather than wedging the cap.
# @label=In-flight spawn TTL (s) @group=spawn @tier=internal @scope=global @edit=int @unit=seconds
#FLEET_INFLIGHT_TTL=180

# --- Cross-machine pre-spawn dedup (bin/dash-issue-session.sh, issue #258) --------
# ON by default (set to 0 to opt out). The local spawn dedup only sees THIS machine's
# tmux server. When two fleets run on DIFFERENT machines against the SAME repo, a
# peer's worker is invisible, so both can spawn issue-<N> — duplicate worktrees, a
# non-fast-forward push race, and competing PRs. With this on, every spawn first
# consults the shared GitHub issue as the claim ledger (assignee · state · a ▶
# claiming comment · an open PR); if the issue is already claimed it refuses. If free
# it claims AT SPAWN (assignee + marker) — not on the worker's first /fleet-claim
# turn, which was the race — then a best-effort tie-break (earliest ▶ claiming
# comment wins) rolls back and refuses if a peer claimed in the same sub-second overlap.
#
# NOT a mutex: GitHub has no compare-and-swap on an issue, so this SHRINKS the
# duplicate-spawn window to a sub-second race, it does not eliminate it. COST: a few
# gh READS per spawn (latency + a little rate limit) — claim-at-spawn only moves the
# worker's own /fleet-claim writes earlier (same write count; /fleet-claim then
# no-ops), and a gh outage/absence degrades to spawn-anyway, never a false refusal. A
# single-machine fleet that wants the zero-gh fast path can set this to 0. Same-account
# caveat: all workers assign the SAME gh account, so a HUMAN self-assigning an issue
# they aren't fleet-working reads as taken and is skipped (arguably correct). Stale
# claim from a dead worker? Spawn past it with `dash-issue-session.sh <N> --force`
# (alias --reclaim).
# @label=Cross-machine pre-spawn dedup @group=spawn @tier=common @scope=fleet @edit=bool
#FLEET_PRESPAWN_DEDUP=1

# --- Auto-merge strategy (issue #283) ---------------------------------------
# The GitHub auto-merge strategy the fleet ARMS. THE FLEET NEVER MERGES — the
# worker's /fleet-claim ship step (and the dash ⌃l arm) run
# `gh pr merge --auto --<method>`; GitHub performs the merge when the PR is green.
# squash (default) | merge | rebase. An unset/typo'd value falls back to squash so
# arming never breaks on a bad key. Per-fleet ▸ global ▸ default.
# @label=Auto-merge method @group=spawn @tier=common @scope=fleet @edit=enum
#FLEET_MERGE_METHOD="squash"

# --- Repo charter gate (issue #283) -----------------------------------------
# When 1, /fleet-claim loads the repo-shipped worker CHARTER from
# $FLEET_MAIN/.fleet/worker.md and layers it UNDER the fleet overlay (operator)
# charter.
# OFF by default (fail-closed): a .fleet/*.md is an INJECTION SURFACE — a PR that
# lands without human review could rewrite the charter every future worker then
# obeys. Opt in ONLY on a repo where you trust what
# lands, and on a public repo protect .fleet/ with CODEOWNERS + required review.
# The fleet OVERLAY charter (~/.config/.../fleets/<session>/worker.md)
# needs no gate — only the operator writes it, and it wins on conflict.
# @label=Load repo charters @group=spawn @tier=advanced @scope=fleet @edit=bool
#FLEET_REPO_CHARTER=0

# --- Tap-first input steer (issue #328) -------------------------------------
# When 1, the worker charter gains a shared "tap-first" block that steers a
# session to OFFER a tappable AskUserQuestion menu (2–4 concrete options,
# recommended-first) instead of an open-ended prose question when it needs a
# BOUNDED decision from the operator — /fleet-claim appends it at worker charter
# load (via fleet_worker_charter).
# OFF by default — most operators are on a real keyboard. Turn it on per machine
# for an operator who drives the fleet from an iPad / Termius soft keyboard, where
# picking an option is ~one keystroke and composing prose is the real friction.
# GUIDANCE, never enforcement: it must not force a menu where free text is correct,
# and must not make sessions ask MORE — it steers HOW a needed question is asked,
# not how often. Spends no extra tokens (charter text only). Per-fleet ▸ global ▸
# default like any @scope=fleet key.
# @label=Tap-first option menus @group=spawn @tier=common @scope=fleet @edit=bool
#FLEET_TAP_FIRST=0

# --- Worker seed prompt (issue #234) ----------------------------------------
# The operator-facing BODY of the one-shot prompt a newly spawned IMPLEMENTING
# worker is seeded with. Since issue #283 the seed COLLAPSES to essentially "run
# /fleet-claim" (the skill carries the whole lifecycle: claim → charter → ground →
# implement → open PR + arm auto-merge). The full seed is STRUCTURAL + body:
#   Work GitHub issue #<n> in this repo. <run /fleet-claim …> <BODY><ship+stop tail>
# Only <BODY> is customizable — the issue-binding head, the /fleet-claim ritual,
# and the "open the PR + arm auto-merge, then STOP" tail are kept intact so the
# fleet machinery still works. (For richer per-fleet standing orders, prefer the
# fleet overlay worker charter — see FLEET_REPO_CHARTER above.) Unset ⇒ the
# built-in default below. Placeholders {issue} and {repo} are substituted; a
# trailing sentence-ender is stripped so the body flows into the tail. Per-fleet ▸
# global ▸ default like any @scope=fleet key.
# A value of the form "@<path>" names a FILE whose contents are the body instead —
# for a long or multi-line template the single-line modal editor can't hold (a
# leading ~/ is expanded; unreadable ⇒ the spawn warns and uses the default). The
# retired FLEET_WORKER_PROMPT_FILE=<path> is still read and wins when set (#1101).
# @label=Worker seed prompt @group=spawn @tier=common @scope=fleet @edit=str
#FLEET_WORKER_PROMPT="Implement and verify per the repo conventions"
